window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-chem-equilibrium","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Equilibrium · Practice","chapter":"Equilibrium","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A bottle of soda water, when left open to the air for some time, turns 'flat'. The best explanation is that:","image":"","options":["CO$_2$ reacts with water completely to form carbonic acid","The solubility of CO$_2$ increases at lower pressure","Dissolved CO$_2$ escapes until a new equilibrium is reached with the much lower partial pressure of CO$_2$ in the atmosphere","Water evaporates and the CO$_2$ crystallises out"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The bottle is sealed while the CO$_2$ pressure above the liquid is high, so a large amount of CO$_2$ is dissolved (Henry's law).\nWhen the bottle is opened, the pressure above the liquid drops to the partial pressure of CO$_2$ in the atmosphere.\nCO$_2$ escapes until the equilibrium CO$_2$(g) ⇌ CO$_2$(aq) is re-established at this lower pressure, and the drink goes flat.\nOption (2) is wrong because solubility decreases, not increases, when pressure falls.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The methanation reaction $CO(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons CH_4(g) + H_2O(g)$ is at equilibrium at a fixed temperature, where $K_c = 3.92\\ L^2\\,mol^{-2}$. The mixture is suddenly compressed to half its volume at the same temperature. What is the value of $Q_c$ immediately after compression, before any reaction occurs?","image":"","options":["3.92 $L^2\\,mol^{-2}$","0.98 $L^2\\,mol^{-2}$","15.68 $L^2\\,mol^{-2}$","1.96 $L^2\\,mol^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: halving the volume doubles every molar concentration, and $K_c = 3.92$.\nFormula: $Q_c = \\frac{[CH_4][H_2O]}{[CO][H_2]^3}$\nSubstituting doubled concentrations: $Q_c = \\frac{(2)(2)}{(2)(2)^3}K_c = \\frac{K_c}{4}$\nSo $Q_c = \\frac{3.92}{4} = 0.98\\ L^2\\,mol^{-2}$.\nSince $Q_c < K_c$, the net reaction proceeds forward, towards fewer gas moles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When an aqueous solution of acetic acid is diluted at constant temperature, its degree of ionization increases.\nReason (R): For a weak acid, $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha}$, and $K_a$ stays constant at a fixed temperature, so lowering $c$ must raise $\\alpha$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a weak acid becomes more ionized as the solution is made more dilute.\nR is true: $K_a$ depends only on temperature. In $K_a = c\\alpha^2/(1-\\alpha)$, a smaller $c$ therefore requires a larger $\\alpha$ (roughly $\\alpha \\approx \\sqrt{K_a/c}$).\nR directly accounts for A, so it is the correct explanation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the equilibrium constant of a reaction are correct?\n(i) Its value does not depend on the initial concentrations of reactants and products.\n(ii) It has one unique value for a given balanced equation at a given temperature.\n(iii) For the reverse reaction, it equals the reciprocal of the forward equilibrium constant.\n(iv) Its expression applies only when the concentrations have become constant at equilibrium.\n(v) Adding a catalyst increases its value because equilibrium is reached faster.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: K is independent of the starting concentrations.\n(ii) Correct: K is temperature dependent, with one value per balanced equation at a given temperature.\n(iii) Correct: $K' = 1/K$ for the reverse reaction.\n(iv) Correct: the K expression uses equilibrium concentrations only.\n(v) Incorrect: a catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally and does not change K.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the Haber process, $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$; $\\Delta H = -92.38$ kJ $mol^{-1}$, consider the following changes made to the system at equilibrium:\n(i) adding $N_2$ at constant volume\n(ii) continuously liquefying and removing $NH_3$\n(iii) raising the temperature\n(iv) adding argon at constant volume\n(v) reducing the volume of the vessel at constant temperature\n(vi) adding iron catalyst\nHow many of these shift the equilibrium in the forward direction?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Shifts forward: the added reactant is consumed.\n(ii) Shifts forward: removing product keeps $Q_c < K_c$.\n(iii) Shifts backward: the reaction is exothermic, so $K$ decreases on heating.\n(iv) No shift: partial pressures are unchanged at constant volume.\n(v) Shifts forward: higher pressure favours fewer gas moles (4 → 2).\n(vi) No shift: a catalyst only speeds up the attainment of equilibrium.\nThree changes shift the equilibrium forward.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $H_2O$, $HCO_3^-$, $HSO_4^-$ and $NH_3$ can each act both as a Brönsted acid and as a Brönsted base.\n(ii) The conjugate base of $NH_3$ is $NH_4^+$.\n(iii) $Mg^{2+}$ and $Co^{3+}$ can act as Lewis acids.\n(iv) In aqueous solution the hydronium ion is further hydrated to species such as $H_5O_2^+$, $H_7O_3^+$ and $H_9O_4^+$.\n(v) An aqueous solution of acetic acid contains mainly acetate ions and hydronium ions.\n(vi) The term 'dissociation' strictly refers to a neutral molecule splitting into ions that did not exist before in the solute.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: all four are amphiprotic. For example, $HCO_3^-$ gives $H_2CO_3$ as its conjugate acid and $CO_3^{2-}$ as its conjugate base.\n(ii) Incorrect: the conjugate base of $NH_3$ is $NH_2^-$; $NH_4^+$ is its conjugate acid.\n(iii) Correct: these electron-deficient cations accept electron pairs.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT lists these hydrated forms of $H_3O^+$.\n(v) Incorrect: acetic acid is less than 5% ionised, so unionised molecules dominate.\n(vi) Incorrect: that describes ionisation. Dissociation is the separation of ions already present in the solid, as in NaCl.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the contact process reaction $2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$, $K_c = 1.7 \\times 10^{26}$. Even so, $V_2O_5$ (or platinum) is used in its industrial manufacture. Why?","image":"","options":["To increase the value of $K_c$ further","To shift the equilibrium towards $SO_3$ by lowering $\\Delta H$","To increase the rate, because the oxidation of $SO_2$ is very slow even though $K$ is large","To remove $SO_3$ from the mixture as soon as it forms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A large K tells us the reaction would go nearly to completion. It gives no information about how fast equilibrium is reached.\nNCERT states that the oxidation of $SO_2$ to $SO_3$ is practically very slow.\nA catalyst provides a lower-energy pathway and speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions.\nA catalyst does not change K, $\\Delta H$ or the equilibrium composition, so options (1) and (2) are wrong.\nIt also does not remove any product, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For CaCO$_3$(s) $\\rightleftharpoons$ CaO(s) + CO$_2$(g), $K_p = 2.00$ at 1100 K. A sample of 10.0 g CaCO$_3$ (molar mass 100 g mol$^{-1}$) is heated to 1100 K in an evacuated, sealed 10.0 L vessel. What is the final pressure of CO$_2$? Take R = 0.0831 bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["2.00 bar","0.914 bar","0.457 bar","1.83 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: n(CaCO$_3$) = 10.0/100 = 0.100 mol.\nTo reach $p_{CO_2}$ = 2.00 bar, the vessel needs n = pV/RT = (2.00 × 10.0)/(0.0831 × 1100) = 0.219 mol CO$_2$.\nOnly 0.100 mol is available, so all the CaCO$_3$ decomposes. With no CaCO$_3$ left, equilibrium is never established.\nSubstituting: p = nRT/V = 0.100 × 0.0831 × 1100/10.0 = 0.914 bar.\nOption (1) wrongly assumes equilibrium is reached.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) An aqueous solution of $NH_4Cl$ is acidic because $NH_4^+$ ions react with water to produce $H_3O^+$.\n(ii) The pH of an acetic acid-sodium acetate buffer changes appreciably when the buffer is diluted with an equal volume of water.\n(iii) The molar solubility of AgCl is lower in aqueous NaCl than in pure water.\n(iv) For a sparingly soluble salt of type $AB_2$ with molar solubility S, $K_{sp} = 4S^3$.\n(v) When a small amount of HCl is added to an acetic acid-sodium acetate buffer, the added $H^+$ is consumed mainly by acetate ions.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $NH_4^+ + H_2O \\rightleftharpoons NH_3 + H_3O^+$, so the solution is acidic.\n(ii) Incorrect: dilution leaves the ratio [salt]/[acid] unchanged, so by $pH = pK_a + \\log\\frac{[salt]}{[acid]}$ the pH stays practically constant.\n(iii) Correct: the common ion $Cl^-$ suppresses the solubility of AgCl.\n(iv) Correct: $K_{sp} = S(2S)^2 = 4S^3$.\n(v) Correct: $CH_3COO^- + H^+ \\rightarrow CH_3COOH$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A soda bottle is sealed at a CO$_2$ partial pressure of 2.5 bar. At this pressure, 0.17 mol of CO$_2$ is dissolved per litre of solution. The bottle is opened and left until equilibrium is reached with air, in which the partial pressure of CO$_2$ is $4 \\times 10^{-4}$ bar. The temperature stays the same. Assuming Henry's law (mass dissolved ∝ partial pressure), what mass of CO$_2$ remains dissolved per litre? (Molar mass of CO$_2$ = 44 g/mol)","image":"","options":["1.20 mg","0.120 mg","12.0 mg","0.0272 mg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 0.17 mol/L of CO$_2$ is dissolved at 2.5 bar. The final pressure is $4\\times10^{-4}$ bar at the same temperature.\nStep 1: Initial mass dissolved = 0.17 × 44 = 7.48 g/L.\nStep 2 (Henry's law): $m_2 = m_1 \\times \\frac{p_2}{p_1} = 7.48 \\times \\frac{4\\times10^{-4}}{2.5}$\nThis gives $m_2 = 1.197\\times10^{-3}$ g ≈ 1.20 mg.\nOption (4), 0.0272 mg, results from forgetting to convert moles to mass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $BF_3$ behaves as an acid when it reacts with $NH_3$, even though it has no proton to donate.\nStatement II: For bases, the Lewis and Brönsted-Lowry concepts differ widely, because a Lewis base need not have a lone pair of electrons.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $BF_3$ is electron deficient. It accepts the lone pair of $NH_3$ to form $BF_3:NH_3$, so it is a Lewis acid even though it has no proton.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT notes that for bases there is little difference between the two concepts, because in both cases the base supplies a lone pair. A Lewis base must donate an electron pair.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a conjugate acid–base pair?","image":"","options":["$H_3O^+$ / $H_2O$","$H_2SO_4$ / $SO_4^{2-}$","$NH_4^+$ / $NH_3$","$HCO_3^-$ / $CO_3^{2-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A conjugate acid–base pair differs by exactly one proton.\n$H_3O^+$/$H_2O$, $NH_4^+$/$NH_3$ and $HCO_3^-$/$CO_3^{2-}$ each differ by one $H^+$.\n$H_2SO_4$ and $SO_4^{2-}$ differ by two protons, so they are not a conjugate pair.\nThe conjugate base of $H_2SO_4$ is $HSO_4^-$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the pH of a 0.10 M aqueous solution of sodium acetate at 298 K. The $pK_a$ of acetic acid is 4.74 and $pK_w = 14$.","image":"","options":["5.13","8.87","9.74","11.13"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: salt of a weak acid and a strong base, c = 0.10 M, $pK_a = 4.74$\nFormula: $pH = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a + \\log c)$\nSubstituting: $pH = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(4.74 + \\log 0.10) = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(4.74 - 1) = 7 + 1.87$\nSo pH = 8.87\nThe answer 5.13 comes from using the formula for a salt of a strong acid and a weak base by mistake.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pH of a $1.0 \\times 10^{-8}$ M HCl solution at 298 K is slightly less than 7, not 8.\nReason (R): In very dilute acid solutions, the $H_3O^+$ produced by the ionisation of water must also be taken into account.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: an acid solution cannot have pH above 7. Solving $x(10^{-8} + x) = 10^{-14}$ gives $x = 9.5 \\times 10^{-8}$ M, so $[H_3O^+] \\approx 1.05 \\times 10^{-7}$ M and pH = 6.98.\nR is true: water's own contribution (about $10^{-7}$ M) is larger than the $10^{-8}$ M from HCl.\nSince including water's ionisation is exactly what gives pH < 7, R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Equal concentrations of the following salts are dissolved separately in water at 298 K. Which solution will have pH greater than 7?","image":"","options":["$NH_4Cl$","$NaCl$","$CH_3COONa$","$KNO_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$CH_3COONa$ is the salt of a weak acid ($CH_3COOH$) and a strong base (NaOH).\nThe acetate ion hydrolyses: $CH_3COO^- + H_2O \\rightleftharpoons CH_3COOH + OH^-$. This releases $OH^-$, so the solution is basic (pH > 7).\n$NH_4Cl$ is the salt of a strong acid and a weak base, so its solution is acidic. It is the tempting wrong choice.\nNaCl and $KNO_3$ come from a strong acid and a strong base. They do not hydrolyse, so their solutions are neutral.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Four reactions have the following values of $K_c$ at the stated temperatures. For which reaction will the equilibrium mixture contain appreciable concentrations of both reactants and products?","image":"","options":["$K_c = 2.4 \\times 10^{47}$","$K_c = 4.8 \\times 10^{-31}$","$K_c = 4.64 \\times 10^{-3}$","$K_c = 5.4 \\times 10^{18}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule of thumb: if $K_c > 10^{3}$, products predominate. If $K_c < 10^{-3}$, reactants predominate. In the range $10^{-3}$ to $10^{3}$, both are present in appreciable amounts.\n$4.64 \\times 10^{-3}$ falls in this range. It is the value for $N_2O_4 \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2$ at 25°C.\n$2.4 \\times 10^{47}$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{18}$ mean the reaction goes nearly to completion.\n$4.8 \\times 10^{-31}$ means the reaction hardly proceeds.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Equimolar (0.1 M) aqueous solutions of four weak monobasic acids are prepared at 298 K. Their ionization constants are: niacin $1.5 \\times 10^{-5}$, acetic acid $1.74 \\times 10^{-5}$, benzoic acid $6.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ and hypochlorous acid $3.0 \\times 10^{-8}$. Which solution has the lowest pH?","image":"","options":["Niacin","Acetic acid","Benzoic acid","Hypochlorous acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At a given temperature, $K_a$ measures acid strength. The larger the $K_a$, the more the acid ionizes.\nAt equal concentration, $[H^+] \\approx \\sqrt{K_a c}$, so the largest $K_a$ gives the largest $[H^+]$ and the lowest pH.\nBenzoic acid has the largest value, $6.5 \\times 10^{-5}$.\nHOCl has the smallest $K_a$, so its solution has the highest pH. Acetic acid is the tempting choice, but its $K_a$ is lower than that of benzoic acid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pH of an aqueous solution of ammonium acetate stays practically the same when the solution is diluted.\nReason (R): For a salt of a weak acid and a weak base, $pH = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a - pK_b)$, which contains no concentration term.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In ammonium acetate, both $NH_4^+$ and $CH_3COO^-$ hydrolyse. The pH depends only on the relative strengths of the parent acid and base.\nR is true. The expression $pH = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a - pK_b)$ contains only constants and no concentration term c.\nBecause no c appears, dilution leaves the pH unchanged. So R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"$N_2O_4$ is placed in a closed rigid vessel at 400 K so that its initial pressure is 5.0 bar. It dissociates as $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$. At equilibrium, the total pressure is 9.0 bar. What is the value of $K_c$ at 400 K? (Take R = 0.0831 bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["64","0.52","1.93","3.85"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: initial pressure of $N_2O_4$ = 5.0 bar; total pressure at equilibrium = 9.0 bar; T = 400 K.\nLet the pressure of $N_2O_4$ fall by x. At equilibrium, $p_{N_2O_4} = 5 - x$ and $p_{NO_2} = 2x$.\nTotal pressure: $(5 - x) + 2x = 9$, so x = 4. This gives $p_{N_2O_4}$ = 1 bar and $p_{NO_2}$ = 8 bar.\n$K_p = \\frac{8^2}{1} = 64$.\n$K_c = \\frac{K_p}{(RT)^{\\Delta n}} = \\frac{64}{0.0831 \\times 400} = 1.93$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For how many of the following equilibria is $K_p = K_c$?\n(i) $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$\n(ii) $N_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO(g)$\n(iii) $CO(g) + H_2O(g) \\rightleftharpoons CO_2(g) + H_2(g)$\n(iv) $PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$\n(v) $CaCO_3(s) \\rightleftharpoons CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$\n(vi) $C(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)$\n(vii) $CO(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons CH_4(g) + H_2O(g)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$. Here $\\Delta n$ counts only gaseous moles, and $K_p = K_c$ only when $\\Delta n = 0$.\n(i) $\\Delta n = 0$; (ii) $\\Delta n = 0$; (iii) $\\Delta n = 0$.\n(iv) $\\Delta n = 1$; (v) $\\Delta n = 1$, since solids are not counted.\n(vi) $\\Delta n = 2 - 1 = 1$; (vii) $\\Delta n = 2 - 4 = -2$.\nOnly three equilibria qualify.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): While calculating $K_p$ for a gaseous reaction, the partial pressures should be expressed in bar.\nReason (R): The standard state for pressure is 1 bar.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that pressure should be expressed in bar when calculating $K_p$.\nR is true: the standard state for pressure is 1 bar.\nR explains A: each pressure is effectively referred to 1 bar. Using the bar unit with $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ keeps $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$ consistent.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Some $N_2O_4$ is placed in a closed vessel at constant temperature, where it exerts an initial pressure of 2.00 bar. It dissociates according to $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$. The total pressure at equilibrium is 3.00 bar. What is the equilibrium partial pressure of $NO_2$?","image":"","options":["1.00 bar","2.00 bar","3.00 bar","4.00 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: initial $p_{N_2O_4}$ = 2.00 bar; at equilibrium, $p_{N_2O_4} = (2.00 - x)$ bar and $p_{NO_2} = 2x$ bar.\nFormula: $p_{total} = (2.00 - x) + 2x = 2.00 + x$.\nSubstituting: $3.00 = 2.00 + x$, so $x = 1.00$ bar.\nSo $p_{NO_2} = 2x = 2.00$ bar.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For a reaction at 300 K, $\\Delta G^\\circ = -5.00$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. At a certain instant the reaction quotient is $Q = 10$. What is $\\Delta G$ at that instant, and in which direction does the net reaction proceed? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\ln 10 = 2.303$)","image":"","options":["$+0.74$ kJ mol$^{-1}$; net reaction proceeds in the reverse direction","$-10.74$ kJ mol$^{-1}$; net reaction proceeds in the forward direction","$-5.00$ kJ mol$^{-1}$; net reaction proceeds in the forward direction","$+5.74$ kJ mol$^{-1}$; net reaction proceeds in the reverse direction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta G^\\circ = -5000$ J mol$^{-1}$, Q = 10, T = 300 K.\nFormula: $\\Delta G = \\Delta G^\\circ + RT\\ln Q$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta G = -5000 + 8.314 \\times 300 \\times 2.303 = -5000 + 5744 = +744$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta G = +0.74$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. A positive value means the net reaction proceeds in the reverse direction.\nThis agrees with $Q > K$, since $K = e^{2.00} \\approx 7.4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which is the correct equilibrium constant expression for $2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$?","image":"","options":["$K_c = \\frac{[SO_3]}{[SO_2][O_2]}$","$K_c = \\frac{[SO_3]^2}{[SO_2]^2[O_2]}$","$K_c = \\frac{[SO_2]^2[O_2]}{[SO_3]^2}$","$K_c = \\frac{2[SO_3]}{2[SO_2][O_2]}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By the law of chemical equilibrium, $K_c$ is the product of product concentrations divided by the product of reactant concentrations.\nEach concentration is raised to the power of its stoichiometric coefficient in the balanced equation.\nThis gives $[SO_3]^2$ in the numerator and $[SO_2]^2[O_2]$ in the denominator.\nOption (3) is the reverse-reaction expression. Coefficients are never used as multipliers, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In Haber's experiment, two ammonia equilibrium mixtures are prepared under identical conditions. One is made with $H_2$ and the other with $D_2$. The two mixtures are then mixed and left for some time. Which observation shows that chemical equilibrium is dynamic?","image":"","options":["The total concentration of ammonia rises sharply after mixing.","The total ammonia concentration stays the same, but a mass spectrometer detects species such as $NH_2D$, $NHD_2$ and $HD$.","Only $NH_3$ and $ND_3$ are found, with no mixed species, because the reaction stops at equilibrium.","All the ammonia decomposes back into $N_2$, $H_2$ and $D_2$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: after mixing, the amount of ammonia is unchanged because the system is still at equilibrium.\nHowever, mass spectrometry shows $NH_3$, $NH_2D$, $NHD_2$, $ND_3$ and $H_2$, $HD$, $D_2$.\nThis scrambling of H and D atoms is only possible if the forward and reverse reactions keep going on at equilibrium.\nOption (3) describes what would happen if the reaction had stopped, which is not what is observed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$, $K_c = 57.0$ at 700 K. At some instant a mixture at 700 K has $[H_2] = 0.050$ M, $[I_2] = 0.050$ M and $[HI] = 0.50$ M. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["$Q_c = 100$; net reaction proceeds in the reverse direction, forming more $H_2$ and $I_2$","$Q_c = 100$; net reaction proceeds in the forward direction, forming more HI","$Q_c = 10$; net reaction proceeds in the forward direction","$Q_c = 10$; net reaction proceeds in the reverse direction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write the reaction quotient: $Q_c = \\frac{[HI]^2}{[H_2][I_2]}$.\nSubstituting: $Q_c = \\frac{(0.50)^2}{(0.050)(0.050)} = \\frac{0.25}{0.0025} = 100$.\nCompare with $K_c$: $Q_c > K_c$ (100 > 57), so the net reaction goes from right to left.\nHI decomposes and $[H_2]$ and $[I_2]$ rise until $Q_c = K_c$.\nThe value 10 comes from forgetting to square [HI].\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In Haber's experiment, one ammonia synthesis was run with H$_2$ and another with D$_2$ under identical conditions. The two equilibrium mixtures were then mixed and left for a while. Which observation proves that equilibrium is dynamic?","image":"","options":["The total concentration of ammonia in the mixture increased after mixing","Only NH$_3$ and ND$_3$ were present in the final mixture","A mass spectrometer detected NH$_2$D, NHD$_2$ and HD along with NH$_3$, ND$_3$, H$_2$ and D$_2$","The total concentration of ammonia in the mixture decreased after mixing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"After mixing, the total ammonia concentration stayed the same as before, so options (1) and (4) are wrong.\nThe mass spectrometer, however, showed mixed species such as NH$_2$D, NHD$_2$ and HD.\nH and D atoms can scramble like this only if the forward and reverse reactions keep going at equilibrium.\nIf the reactions had stopped, only NH$_3$ and ND$_3$ would remain, which is the tempting option (2).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A closed vessel contains CaCO$_3$(s), CaO(s) and CO$_2$(g) at equilibrium at a fixed temperature. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The equilibrium pressure of CO$_2$ does not depend on the amount of CaCO$_3$ taken, provided some solid remains.\n(ii) If CaO were completely absent, the system could not be at equilibrium.\n(iii) $K_p$ increases if the vessel volume is increased while both solids remain present.\n(iv) If the volume is halved (both solids still present), $p_{CO_2}$ at the new equilibrium equals its original value.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $p_{CO_2} = K_p$ depends only on temperature.\n(ii) Correct: a heterogeneous equilibrium requires every pure solid to be present, however small its amount.\n(iii) Incorrect: $K_p$ changes only with temperature.\n(iv) Correct: after compression, CO$_2$ combines with CaO until $p_{CO_2}$ returns to $K_p$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$, $K_c = 4.64 \\times 10^{-3}$ at 25 °C. At some instant a mixture at 25 °C contains $[N_2O_4] = 0.20$ M and $[NO_2] = 0.020$ M. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["$Q_c = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$; net reaction proceeds forward and $[NO_2]$ increases","$Q_c = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$; net reaction proceeds backward and $[N_2O_4]$ increases","$Q_c = 0.10$; net reaction proceeds backward and $[N_2O_4]$ increases","$Q_c = K_c$; the mixture is already at equilibrium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $Q_c = \\frac{[NO_2]^2}{[N_2O_4]}$.\nSubstituting: $Q_c = \\frac{(0.020)^2}{0.20} = \\frac{4.0 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.20} = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$.\nCompare: $Q_c (2.0 \\times 10^{-3}) < K_c (4.64 \\times 10^{-3})$, so net reaction goes left to right and more $NO_2$ forms.\nThe value 0.10 comes from forgetting to square $[NO_2]$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A pH paper with four strips, each showing a different colour at the same pH, is used to find the pH of solutions in the range 1–14. Roughly what accuracy can be obtained this way?","image":"","options":["About 0.001 pH unit","About 0.05 pH unit","About 2 pH units","About 0.5 pH unit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that four-strip pH paper gives pH in the range 1–14 with an accuracy of about 0.5.\nThe precision of about 0.001 belongs to a pH meter, not pH paper. A pH meter measures the pH-dependent electrical potential of the test solution.\nSo pH paper is a rough method, and pH meters are used when greater accuracy is needed.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Adding more solid CaCO$_3$ to an equilibrium mixture of CaCO$_3$(s), CaO(s) and CO$_2$(g) in a closed vessel at constant temperature does not change the pressure of CO$_2$.\nReason (R): The concentration of a pure solid is independent of the amount present, so it does not appear in the equilibrium constant expression.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. At a given temperature $K_p = p_{CO_2}$, so the CO$_2$ pressure is fixed. Adding solid does not change it.\nR is true. [CaCO$_3$(s)] is constant and is absorbed into K.\nR explains A. Since the solid's amount does not enter the expression, changing that amount leaves $p_{CO_2}$ unchanged.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The molar solubility of calcium phosphate, $Ca_3(PO_4)_2$, in water is S mol $L^{-1}$. Its solubility product $K_{sp}$ is:","image":"","options":["$108\\,S^5$","$6\\,S^5$","$27\\,S^4$","$36\\,S^5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Dissociation: $Ca_3(PO_4)_2(s) \\rightleftharpoons 3Ca^{2+} + 2PO_4^{3-}$\nFor a molar solubility S: $[Ca^{2+}] = 3S$ and $[PO_4^{3-}] = 2S$.\n$K_{sp} = (3S)^3(2S)^2 = 27S^3 \\times 4S^2 = 108\\,S^5$\nThe option $6S^5$ multiplies the coefficients instead of raising them to the stoichiometric powers.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the equilibrium $PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$, $K_c = 0.040$ at 500 K. What is $K_p$ at the same temperature? (Take R = 0.0831 bar L $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["1.66","0.040","$9.6 \\times 10^{-4}$","41.6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_c = 0.040$, T = 500 K, R = 0.0831 bar L $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$.\nFind $\\Delta n$: moles of gaseous products minus gaseous reactants = (1 + 1) − 1 = 1.\nFormula: $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$\nSubstituting: $K_p = 0.040 \\times (0.0831 \\times 500)^1 = 0.040 \\times 41.55$\nSo $K_p \\approx 1.66$.\nThe option 0.040 assumes $\\Delta n = 0$, which is wrong here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In the NCERT experiment, the equilibrium $Fe^{3+}(aq) + SCN^-(aq) \\rightleftharpoons [Fe(SCN)]^{2+}(aq)$ gives a deep red solution. Which pair of reagents will both reduce the intensity of the red colour when added separately to the equilibrium mixture?","image":"","options":["Potassium thiocyanate and mercury(II) chloride","Oxalic acid and mercury(II) chloride","Iron(III) nitrate and oxalic acid","Potassium thiocyanate and iron(III) nitrate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oxalic acid binds $Fe^{3+}$ as the stable complex $[Fe(C_2O_4)_3]^{3-}$. The loss of free $Fe^{3+}$ shifts the equilibrium to the left, so the red colour fades.\nAqueous $HgCl_2$ binds $SCN^-$ as $[Hg(SCN)_4]^{2-}$. This also shifts the equilibrium to the left, so the colour fades.\nKSCN and $Fe(NO_3)_3$ add a reactant. That shifts the equilibrium to the right and deepens the red colour.\nThe other three options each contain at least one of these colour-deepening reagents.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"When sodium chloride dissolves in water, its ions separate and move freely. According to NCERT, which property of water is mainly responsible for weakening the electrostatic attraction between $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ ions?","image":"","options":["Its high dielectric constant of about 80, which reduces the interionic force about 80 times","Its high density of $1000\\ g\\ L^{-1}$, which pushes the ions apart","Its ability to ionise into $H_3O^+$ and $OH^-$, which neutralise the ions","Its low molar mass of 18 g/mol, which lets many molecules surround each ion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The electrostatic force between two charges is inversely proportional to the dielectric constant of the medium.\nWater has a very high dielectric constant (about 80), so the attraction between $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ falls by a factor of about 80.\nHydration by polar water molecules then keeps the ions well separated.\nThe self-ionisation of water ($[H^+]=10^{-7}$ M) is far too small to matter, and density or molar mass does not control interionic force.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"During a titration at 298 K, the pH of a solution rises from 3.0 to 5.0. By what factor has the hydrogen ion concentration of the solution decreased?","image":"","options":["2","20","100","1000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: initial pH = 3.0, final pH = 5.0\nFormula: $[H^+] = 10^{-pH}$, so the factor = $\\frac{10^{-3}}{10^{-5}}$\nSubstituting: factor = $10^{5-3} = 10^2$\nThe pH scale is logarithmic. A change of 1 pH unit means a 10-fold change in $[H^+]$, so a change of 2 units means a 100-fold change. The value 2 comes from wrongly treating pH as a linear scale.\nSo $[H^+]$ decreases by a factor of 100.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Two sealed test tubes contain the same brown mixture for the equilibrium $2NO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons N_2O_4(g)$; $\\Delta H = -57.2$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. One tube is placed in a freezing mixture and the other in hot water at 363 K. Which observation is expected?","image":"","options":["The tube in the freezing mixture becomes paler and the tube in hot water becomes deeper brown","The tube in the freezing mixture becomes deeper brown and the tube in hot water becomes paler","Both tubes become paler because the value of $K_c$ does not depend on temperature","Neither tube changes colour because the total number of moles is constant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NO$_2$ is brown and N$_2$O$_4$ is colourless.\nThe forward dimerisation is exothermic.\nOn cooling, the system favours the exothermic forward reaction. Less NO$_2$ remains, so the colour fades.\nOn heating, the endothermic reverse reaction is favoured. More NO$_2$ forms, so the brown colour deepens.\nOption 3 fails because K does change with temperature. Option 4 fails because the number of gas moles changes from 2 to 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the equilibrium $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$, $\\Delta H = -92.38$ kJ/mol. How many of the following changes will shift this equilibrium in the forward direction?\n(a) Compressing the mixture to half its volume\n(b) Raising the temperature\n(c) Adding an iron catalyst\n(d) Liquefying and removing $NH_3$\n(e) Adding argon at constant volume\n(f) Adding more $H_2$\n(g) Lowering the temperature","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Yes. The forward reaction reduces gaseous moles (4 → 2), so compression favours it.\n(b) No. The reaction is exothermic, so heating shifts it backward and lowers K.\n(c) No. A catalyst only speeds up attainment of equilibrium.\n(d) Yes. Removing the product keeps $Q_c < K_c$.\n(e) No. Partial pressures are unchanged.\n(f) Yes. Added reactant is consumed by the forward reaction.\n(g) Yes. Lowering the temperature favours the exothermic direction.\nThat gives 4 changes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The chloride ion, $Cl^-$, is a much weaker base than water.\nReason (R): HCl is a strong acid that is almost completely ionised in water, and a strong acid has a very weak conjugate base.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT lists $Cl^-$, along with $ClO_4^-$, $Br^-$, $I^-$, $NO_3^-$ and $HSO_4^-$, as bases much weaker than $H_2O$.\nR is true. HCl donates its proton readily, so its conjugate base $Cl^-$ has almost no tendency to take the proton back. In general, a strong acid has a very weak conjugate base.\nThe weakness of $Cl^-$ follows directly from the strength of HCl, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For a weak monobasic acid HA of concentration $c$ and ionization constant $K_a$ (with $\\alpha \\ll 1$), match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $K_a$\n(B) $K_b$ of $A^-$\n(C) pH of the solution\n(D) Degree of ionization $\\alpha$\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{K_w}{K_a}$\n(ii) $c\\alpha^2$\n(iii) $\\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{c}}$\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{2}(pK_a - \\log c)$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) When $\\alpha \\ll 1$, $1 - \\alpha \\approx 1$, so $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha}$ reduces to $c\\alpha^2$: A-(ii).\n(B) $K_a K_b = K_w$, so $K_b = K_w/K_a$: B-(i).\n(C) $[H^+] = c\\alpha = \\sqrt{K_a c}$, so pH $= \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a - \\log c)$: C-(iv).\n(D) From $K_a = c\\alpha^2$, $\\alpha = \\sqrt{K_a/c}$: D-(iii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.10 M solution of a weak monobasic acid HA has $K_a = 1.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ at 298 K. Assuming $\\alpha \\ll 1$, what is the percent dissociation of the acid?","image":"","options":["0.1 %","1 %","10 %","0.01 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $c = 0.10$ M and $K_a = 1.0 \\times 10^{-5}$.\nFormula: $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha} \\approx c\\alpha^2$, so $\\alpha = \\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{c}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\alpha = \\sqrt{\\frac{10^{-5}}{0.1}} = \\sqrt{10^{-4}} = 0.01$.\nPercent dissociation $= \\alpha \\times 100 = 1$ %.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the contact process reaction $2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$, $K_c = 1.7 \\times 10^{26}$. Why is $V_2O_5$ (or platinum) still used in the industrial process?","image":"","options":["To increase the value of $K_c$ further","Because the oxidation of $SO_2$ to $SO_3$ is very slow even though $K$ is very large","To shift the equilibrium towards $SO_2$ and $O_2$","Because a catalyst is required to make a reaction with a very small $K$ go to completion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A very large $K$ means the reaction should go almost to completion, but $K$ says nothing about the rate.\nNCERT notes that in practice the oxidation of $SO_2$ to $SO_3$ is very slow, so $V_2O_5$ or Pt is used to increase the rate.\nA catalyst never changes $K$, which rules out option (1).\nFor a reaction with an exceedingly small $K$, a catalyst would be of little help, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ionic product of water, $K_w$, does not have the same value at all temperatures.\nReason (R): The concentration of water (about 55.55 M) is treated as constant and absorbed into $K_w$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: $K_w$ is an equilibrium constant, and equilibrium constants depend on temperature.\nR is true: water is a pure liquid, so its nearly constant concentration is included within $K_w = [H^+][OH^-]$.\nHowever, R only explains why $[H_2O]$ is missing from the expression. It does not explain why $K_w$ varies with temperature.\nSo both statements are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"2.00 mol of $PCl_5$ is heated in a closed 1 L vessel and the reaction $PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$ is allowed to reach equilibrium. At equilibrium, 0.80 mol of $PCl_5$ remains. What is the degree of dissociation of $PCl_5$?","image":"","options":["0.40","0.60","0.80","1.20"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: initial $PCl_5$ = 2.00 mol, equilibrium $PCl_5$ = 0.80 mol.\nMoles dissociated = 2.00 − 0.80 = 1.20 mol.\nFormula: $\\alpha$ = moles dissociated / initial moles.\nSubstituting: $\\alpha = 1.20/2.00 = 0.60$.\nThe tempting value 0.40 is the fraction left undissociated, not the fraction dissociated.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The Arrhenius concept cannot explain the basic nature of an aqueous solution of ammonia because:","image":"","options":["$NH_3$ does not contain an $OH$ group, yet its solution is basic","$NH_3$ is completely ionised in water","$NH_3$ donates a proton to water","$NH_3$ is insoluble in water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"According to Arrhenius, a base is a substance that releases $OH^-$ ions of its own in water, like MOH.\n$NH_3$ has no hydroxyl group, yet its aqueous solution is basic.\nThe Brönsted–Lowry concept explains this: $NH_3$ accepts a proton from water, which produces $OH^-$.\nOption 3 is wrong because $NH_3$ accepts a proton from water rather than donating one.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For H$_2$(g) + I$_2$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2HI(g), $K_c$ = 64 at a certain temperature. Match each reaction in List-I with its $K_c$ at the same temperature in List-II.\nList-I\n(P) 2HI(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ H$_2$(g) + I$_2$(g)\n(Q) $\\frac{1}{2}$H$_2$(g) + $\\frac{1}{2}$I$_2$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ HI(g)\n(R) HI(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ $\\frac{1}{2}$H$_2$(g) + $\\frac{1}{2}$I$_2$(g)\n(S) 2H$_2$(g) + 2I$_2$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 4HI(g)\nList-II\n(1) 8\n(2) $\\frac{1}{64}$\n(3) 4096\n(4) $\\frac{1}{8}$","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3","P-2, Q-1, R-3, S-4","P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(P) This is the reverse reaction, so $K = \\frac{1}{64}$ → (2).\n(Q) The equation is halved, so $K = 64^{1/2} = 8$ → (1).\n(R) This is the halved equation reversed, so $K = \\frac{1}{8}$ → (4).\n(S) The equation is doubled, so $K = 64^2 = 4096$ → (3).\nThis gives P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction $CO(g) + H_2O(g) \\rightleftharpoons CO_2(g) + H_2(g)$, $K_c = 9.0$ at a certain temperature. Initially only CO and $H_2O$ are present, each at a concentration of 0.20 M. What is the equilibrium concentration of $CO_2$?","image":"","options":["0.050 M","0.10 M","0.15 M","0.18 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: at equilibrium, $[CO] = [H_2O] = (0.20 - x)$ and $[CO_2] = [H_2] = x$.\nFormula: $K_c = \\frac{x^2}{(0.20-x)^2} = 9.0$.\nTaking the square root: $\\frac{x}{0.20 - x} = 3$, so $x = 0.60 - 3x$, which gives $4x = 0.60$.\nSo $x = 0.15$ M. Hence $[CO_2] = 0.15$ M and $[CO] = 0.050$ M.\nThe option 0.050 M is the equilibrium [CO], not $[CO_2]$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the equilibrium $C(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)$, the total pressure is increased by compressing the mixture at constant temperature. In which direction does the equilibrium shift?","image":"","options":["Forward, because solid carbon is consumed","No shift, because carbon is a solid","Forward, because $K_p$ increases with pressure","Backward, because the forward reaction increases the number of moles of gas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Only gaseous species count when judging the effect of pressure. The volume of a solid is nearly independent of pressure.\nThere is 1 mol of gas on the left and 2 mol of gas on the right.\nBy Le Chatelier's principle, a rise in pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles. So the reaction goes in the reverse direction.\nK depends only on temperature, so option (3) is wrong.\nThe gas moles do change in this reaction, so option (2) is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $H_2CO_3$ is the conjugate base of $HCO_3^-$.\nStatement II: $NH_2^-$ is a much stronger base than $H_2O$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A conjugate base has one proton less, so the conjugate base of $HCO_3^-$ is $CO_3^{2-}$. $H_2CO_3$, which has one extra proton, is its conjugate acid.\nStatement II is correct. $NH_3$ is a very weak acid, so its conjugate base $NH_2^-$ is a very good proton acceptor, far stronger than water.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At a certain temperature, $K_c$ for $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$ is 64. What is $K_c$ for $NH_3(g) \\rightleftharpoons \\frac{1}{2}N_2(g) + \\frac{3}{2}H_2(g)$ at the same temperature?","image":"","options":["8","0.125","0.0156","0.0313"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $K_c$ = 64 for the forward reaction.\nStep 1: Reversing the equation gives $\\frac{1}{K_c} = \\frac{1}{64}$.\nStep 2: Halving the reversed equation raises the constant to the power 1/2.\nSubstituting: $K = (64)^{-1/2} = \\frac{1}{8}$.\nSo $K$ = 0.125. Reversing without halving gives 0.0156, and halving without reversing gives 8.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, $K_a$ of HF is $3.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ and $K_a$ of HCOOH is $1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nStatement I: The base ionization constant $K_b$ of $F^-$ is greater than that of $HCOO^-$.\nStatement II: For any conjugate acid-base pair in water, $K_a \\times K_b = K_w$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. Adding the acid and base ionization equilibria of a conjugate pair gives water autoionization, so $K_a K_b = K_w$.\nBy this relation, $K_b = \\frac{K_w}{K_a}$. The stronger acid therefore has the weaker conjugate base.\nHF has the larger $K_a$, so $K_b(F^-) = 2.9 \\times 10^{-11}$. This is smaller than $K_b(HCOO^-) = 5.6 \\times 10^{-11}$.\nSo Statement I is incorrect.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 1100 K, $CaCO_3(s) \\rightleftharpoons CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$ is at equilibrium in a closed vessel with $p_{CO_2} = 2.0$ bar, and both solids are present. The volume of the vessel is then halved at the same temperature, and both solids remain present. What is the partial pressure of $CO_2$ after equilibrium is re-established?","image":"","options":["4.0 bar","1.0 bar","2.0 bar","2.83 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $K_p = p_{CO_2} = 2.0$ at 1100 K, since pure solids are omitted from the expression.\nHalving the volume momentarily raises $p_{CO_2}$ to 4.0 bar. Now $Q_p > K_p$, so $CO_2$ combines with CaO and the reaction shifts backward.\nThe shift continues until $p_{CO_2}$ again equals $K_p$, which is unchanged because the temperature is the same.\nSo $p_{CO_2} = 2.0$ bar; only the amount of $CO_2$ gas decreases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For a reaction at a given temperature, the standard Gibbs energy change $\\Delta G^\\circ$ is negative. Which of the following correctly describes its equilibrium constant $K$?","image":"","options":["$K > 1$; products predominate at equilibrium","$K < 1$; reactants predominate at equilibrium","$K = 1$; reactants and products are equal at equilibrium","$K = 0$; no product is formed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"From $K = e^{-\\Delta G^\\circ/RT}$: if $\\Delta G^\\circ < 0$, the exponent $-\\Delta G^\\circ/RT$ is positive.\nThe exponential of a positive number is greater than 1, so $K > 1$.\nThe forward reaction then proceeds far enough that products are present predominantly.\n$K < 1$ applies when $\\Delta G^\\circ > 0$. $K = 1$ needs $\\Delta G^\\circ = 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At a temperature where the ionic product of water is $K_w = 1.0 \\times 10^{-13}$, pure water has a pH of 6.5 and is still neutral.\nReason (R): An aqueous solution is neutral when $[H_3O^+] = [OH^-]$, whatever the value of $K_w$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Checking A: $K_w$ is an equilibrium constant, so it depends on temperature. In pure water, $[H^+] = [OH^-] = \\sqrt{10^{-13}} = 10^{-6.5}$ M, so pH = 6.5. Since the two concentrations are still equal, the water is neutral. A is true.\nChecking R: Neutrality is defined by $[H_3O^+] = [OH^-]$, not by pH = 7. The value pH = 7 marks neutrality only at 298 K. R is true.\nR explains why water with pH 6.5 is still neutral.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which condition correctly identifies a neutral aqueous solution at any temperature?","image":"","options":["$[H_3O^+] = [OH^-]$","pH = 7","$[H_3O^+] = 1.0\\times10^{-7}$ M","pOH = 7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines solutions by comparing ion concentrations. Acidic: $[H_3O^+] > [OH^-]$. Neutral: $[H_3O^+] = [OH^-]$. Basic: $[H_3O^+] < [OH^-]$.\nThe conditions pH = 7, pOH = 7 and $[H^+] = 10^{-7}$ M hold only at 298 K, where $K_w = 10^{-14}$.\n$K_w$ changes with temperature, so these numerical values also change.\nOnly equality of the two ion concentrations stays valid at every temperature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A reaction has an equilibrium constant $K = 10$ at 300 K. What is its standard Gibbs energy change $\\Delta G^\\circ$? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\ln 10 = 2.303$)","image":"","options":["$-5.74$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+5.74$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-2.49$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-57.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: K = 10, T = 300 K, R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta G^\\circ = -8.314 \\times 300 \\times 2.303 = -5744$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta G^\\circ = -5.74$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. The negative sign fits $K > 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the pH of a $1.0\\times10^{-7}$ M HCl solution at 298 K. Take $K_w = 1.0\\times10^{-14}$, $\\sqrt{5} = 2.236$ and $\\log 1.618 = 0.209$. Include the $H_3O^+$ coming from water.","image":"","options":["7.00","6.79","6.70","7.21"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: [HCl] $= 10^{-7}$ M. The acid is very dilute, so the ionization of water cannot be ignored.\nLet $x = [OH^-]$, which also equals the $H_3O^+$ contributed by water. Then $[H_3O^+] = 10^{-7} + x$.\nFormula: $(10^{-7} + x)x = 10^{-14}$, which gives $x^2 + 10^{-7}x - 10^{-14} = 0$.\nSolving: $x = \\frac{(-1 + 2.236)\\times10^{-7}}{2} = 0.618\\times10^{-7}$ M.\nSo $[H_3O^+] = 1.618\\times10^{-7}$ M and pH $= 7 - 0.209 = 6.79$.\nA pH of 7.00 ignores the acid, and 6.70 wrongly adds $10^{-7}$ from water without letting the equilibrium adjust.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A sparingly soluble salt precipitates from a solution when the ionic product of its ions exceeds its solubility product $K_{sp}$.\nStatement II: When the ionic product of a sparingly soluble salt is less than its $K_{sp}$, the solution is supersaturated.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. When the ionic product (Q) is greater than $K_{sp}$, the solution holds more ions than it can at equilibrium, so the excess separates out as a precipitate.\nStatement II is incorrect. When Q is less than $K_{sp}$, the solution is unsaturated, and more solid can dissolve.\nWhen Q equals $K_{sp}$, the solution is just saturated.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Adding a catalyst to a reversible reaction at equilibrium increases the equilibrium concentration of the products.\nStatement II: A catalyst lowers the activation energy of the forward and the reverse reactions by exactly the same amount.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally. It only helps equilibrium to be reached faster and does not change the equilibrium composition.\nStatement II is correct. The catalyst provides a new lower-energy pathway through the same transition state for both directions, so both activation energies drop equally.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, an aqueous NaOH solution has $[OH^-] = 1.0\\times10^{-4}$ M. Take $K_w = 1.0\\times10^{-14}$. What is its pH?","image":"","options":["10","4","9","11"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $[OH^-] = 10^{-4}$ M and $K_w = 10^{-14}$ at 298 K.\nFormula: $[H_3O^+] = \\frac{K_w}{[OH^-]}$ and pH $= -\\log[H_3O^+]$.\nSubstituting: $[H_3O^+] = \\frac{10^{-14}}{10^{-4}} = 10^{-10}$ M.\nSo pH = 10. The value 4 is the pOH, not the pH.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about dimensionless equilibrium constants is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The standard state for a pure gas is 1 bar.","The standard state for a solute is a 1 M solution.","A pressure of 4 bar, referred to its standard state, is expressed as the dimensionless number 4.","When $K_p$ and $K_c$ are both expressed as dimensionless quantities, they always have the same numerical value even if $\\Delta n \\neq 0$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT takes 1 bar as the standard state of a pure gas.\n(2) Correct: the standard state for a solute is 1 M.\n(3) Correct: 4 bar / 1 bar = 4, a pure number.\n(4) Incorrect: dimensionless $K_p$ and $K_c$ still have different numerical values, because their standard states differ (1 bar versus 1 M).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The reaction quotient $Q_c$ has the same form as $K_c$, but the concentrations used in it need not be equilibrium values.\n(ii) If $Q_c > K_c$, the net reaction proceeds towards the products.\n(iii) If $Q_c = K_c$, no net reaction occurs.\n(iv) Continuous removal of a gaseous product keeps $Q_c$ below $K_c$, so the reaction keeps moving forward.\n(v) For $2A \\rightleftharpoons B + C$ with $K_c = 2 \\times 10^{-3}$, a mixture having $[A] = [B] = [C] = 5 \\times 10^{-4}$ M will react in the forward direction.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the definition of $Q_c$.\n(ii) Incorrect: when $Q_c > K_c$, the net reaction goes towards the reactants.\n(iii) Correct: the mixture is already at equilibrium.\n(iv) Correct: an example is the removal of $CO_2$ from the lime kiln.\n(v) Incorrect: $Q_c = \\frac{(5\\times10^{-4})^2}{(5\\times10^{-4})^2} = 1 > 2\\times10^{-3}$, so the reaction proceeds in reverse.\nCorrect statements: 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (physical equilibrium) with List-II (quantity that is constant at equilibrium).\nList-I\n(A) $H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons H_2O(g)$\n(B) $H_2O(s) \\rightleftharpoons H_2O(l)$\n(C) Sugar(s) $\\rightleftharpoons$ Sugar(solution)\n(D) $CO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons CO_2(aq)$\nList-II\n(I) Melting point is fixed at constant pressure\n(II) $[CO_2(aq)]/[CO_2(g)]$ is constant at a given temperature\n(III) Vapour pressure of water is constant at a given temperature\n(IV) Concentration of solute in solution is constant at a given temperature","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Liquid–vapour: the equilibrium vapour pressure is constant at a given temperature, so A–III.\n(B) Solid–liquid: the two phases coexist only at the melting point at a given pressure, so B–I.\n(C) Saturated sugar solution: the solubility (solute concentration) is constant at a given temperature, so C–IV.\n(D) Gas dissolved in a liquid (Henry's law): $[CO_2(aq)]/[CO_2(g)]$ is constant, so D–II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the percent dissociation of formic acid in a 0.05 M aqueous solution at 298 K. ($K_a$ of HCOOH $= 1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$; solve without assuming $1 - \\alpha \\approx 1$.)","image":"","options":["0.36 %","2.9 %","11.6 %","5.8 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $c = 0.05$ M, $K_a = 1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nFormula: $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha}$, which rearranges to $c\\alpha^2 + K_a\\alpha - K_a = 0$.\nSubstituting: $0.05\\alpha^2 + 1.8 \\times 10^{-4}\\alpha - 1.8 \\times 10^{-4} = 0$.\nTaking the positive root: $\\alpha = \\frac{-1.8\\times10^{-4} + \\sqrt{3.24\\times10^{-8} + 3.6\\times10^{-5}}}{0.1} \\approx 0.058$.\nSo percent dissociation $= 0.058 \\times 100 \\approx 5.8\\%$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"9.2 g of $N_2O_4$ is placed in an evacuated 1.0 L vessel at 400 K and allowed to reach equilibrium: $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$. The total pressure at equilibrium is 4.50 bar. Calculate $K_c$. (Molar mass of $N_2O_4$ = 92 g/mol, $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["0.0775","2.58","0.0388","0.155"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n = 9.2/92 = 0.10 mol, so the initial pressure $p_0 = nRT/V$ = 0.10 × 0.0831 × 400 = 3.324 bar.\nAt equilibrium, $p_{N_2O_4} = 3.324 - x$ and $p_{NO_2} = 2x$.\nTotal pressure: 3.324 + x = 4.50, so x = 1.176 bar.\nPartial pressures: $p_{NO_2}$ = 2.352 bar, $p_{N_2O_4}$ = 2.148 bar.\n$K_p = \\frac{(2.352)^2}{2.148} = 2.58$.\n$K_c = \\frac{K_p}{RT} = \\frac{2.58}{33.24} = 0.0775$. Option 2.58 is $K_p$, not $K_c$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Adding a catalyst to a reaction mixture at equilibrium does not change the equilibrium composition.\nReason (R): A catalyst lowers the activation energy of the forward and the reverse reactions by exactly the same amount.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A catalyst does not affect the equilibrium composition, and it does not appear in the equilibrium constant expression.\nR is true. The catalyst provides a new, lower-energy pathway through the same transition state, so both activation energies fall by the same amount.\nBecause the forward and reverse rates rise equally, equilibrium is only reached faster and does not shift. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (equilibrium and disturbance) with List-II (result).\nList-I:\n(A) $CO(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons CH_4(g) + H_2O(g)$; pressure increased\n(B) $C(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)$; pressure increased\n(C) $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$; volume halved\n(D) $2NO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons N_2O_4(g)$, $\\Delta H = -57.2$ kJ $mol^{-1}$; heated\nList-II:\n(i) Shifts backward because the forward reaction increases the moles of gas\n(ii) No shift in equilibrium\n(iii) Shifts forward because the moles of gas decrease\n(iv) Shifts backward because $K$ decreases","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: 4 mol of gas become 2 mol, so higher pressure shifts it forward → (iii).\nB: 1 mol of gas becomes 2 mol (solid C is ignored), so higher pressure shifts it backward → (i).\nC: $\\Delta n_g = 0$, so halving the volume causes no shift → (ii).\nD: the forward reaction is exothermic, so heating lowers $K$ and favours $NO_2$ → (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The standard Gibbs energy change for a biochemical reaction is $+11.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ at 298 K. What is its equilibrium constant? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $e^{-4.60} \\approx 1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{2}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{-4}$","$2.5 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta G^\\circ = 11400$ J mol$^{-1}$, T = 298 K.\nFormula: $\\ln K = -\\Delta G^\\circ/RT$.\nSubstituting: $\\ln K = -11400/(8.314 \\times 298) = -4.60$.\nSo $K = e^{-4.60} \\approx 1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$, which is less than 1 as expected for positive $\\Delta G^\\circ$.\nOption (4) comes from wrongly using $10^{-4.60}$ in place of $e^{-4.60}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At temperature T, $K_c$ for $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$ is 64. Pure HI at an initial concentration of 1.0 M is placed in a sealed vessel at T and allowed to reach equilibrium. What is the equilibrium concentration of $H_2$?","image":"","options":["0.80 M","0.125 M","0.10 M","0.20 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: equilibrium is approached from the HI side, $2HI \\rightleftharpoons H_2 + I_2$, so $K' = \\frac{1}{64}$.\nLet $[H_2] = [I_2] = x$. Then $[HI] = 1.0 - 2x$.\nFormula: $\\frac{x^2}{(1-2x)^2} = \\frac{1}{64}$, so $\\frac{x}{1-2x} = \\frac{1}{8}$.\nSubstituting: $8x = 1 - 2x$, which gives $x = 0.10$.\nSo $[H_2]$ = 0.10 M. The value 0.80 M is $[HI]$, not $[H_2]$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A few crystals of radioactive sugar are dropped into a saturated solution of ordinary (non-radioactive) sugar that also contains excess undissolved sugar. The temperature is kept constant. Which of the following observations is NOT expected?","image":"","options":["After some time, radioactivity is detected both in the solution and in the solid sugar","The ratio of radioactive to non-radioactive sugar molecules in the solution increases and then becomes constant","The total concentration of dissolved sugar in the solution increases steadily with time","The process shows that the rate of dissolution equals the rate of crystallisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Expected: molecules are exchanged between the solid and the solution, so radioactivity appears in both phases.\n(2) Expected: NCERT states that this ratio rises until it attains a constant value.\n(3) Not expected: the solution is already saturated, and solubility is constant at a given temperature. The total dissolved concentration therefore stays the same; only exchange of molecules occurs.\n(4) Expected: this experiment confirms the dynamic equality of the two rates.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.01 M aqueous solution of a weak monobasic acid HA has $K_a = 1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ at 298 K. What is the approximate pH of the solution? (Neglect the contribution of water.)","image":"","options":["4.0","2.0","3.0","6.0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $c = 0.01$ M, $K_a = 1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$.\nFormula: for small $\\alpha$, $K_a \\approx c\\alpha^2$, so $\\alpha = \\sqrt{K_a/c}$ and $[H^+] = c\\alpha$.\nSubstituting: $\\alpha = \\sqrt{10^{-6}/10^{-2}} = 10^{-2}$, so $[H^+] = 0.01 \\times 0.01 = 10^{-4}$ M.\nSo pH $= -\\log(10^{-4}) = 4.0$. Option (2), pH 2, wrongly treats the acid as fully ionized.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A buffer solution contains 0.02 M acetic acid and 0.20 M sodium acetate. The $pK_a$ of acetic acid is 4.76. What is the pH of the buffer?","image":"","options":["3.76","4.76","5.76","6.76"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $pK_a = 4.76$, [salt] = 0.20 M, [acid] = 0.02 M\nFormula (Henderson-Hasselbalch): $pH = pK_a + \\log\\frac{[salt]}{[acid]}$\nSubstituting: $pH = 4.76 + \\log\\frac{0.20}{0.02} = 4.76 + \\log 10 = 4.76 + 1$\nSo pH = 5.76\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The equilibrium constant of an exothermic reaction decreases as the temperature increases.","For $C(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)$, increasing the pressure shifts the equilibrium in the reverse direction.","In the equilibrium between the pink aqua complex of cobalt and blue $[CoCl_4]^{2-}$, cooling the mixture in a freezing mixture makes it turn deeper blue.","Adding oxalic acid to the deep red $Fe^{3+}/SCN^-/[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$ equilibrium mixture decreases the intensity of the red colour."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: for negative $\\Delta H$, K falls as T rises.\n(2) Correct: gaseous moles increase in the forward direction (1 to 2), so higher pressure favours the reverse reaction.\n(3) Incorrect: formation of $[CoCl_4]^{2-}$ is endothermic. Cooling therefore shifts the equilibrium back to the pink aqua complex, so the mixture turns pink, not deeper blue.\n(4) Correct: oxalate binds $Fe^{3+}$ as $[Fe(C_2O_4)_3]^{3-}$. $[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$ then dissociates to replace $Fe^{3+}$, and the red colour fades.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For how many of the following reactions is $K_p = K_c$?\n$H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$\n$N_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO(g)$\n$PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$\n$2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$\n$C(s) + CO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)$\n$CO(g) + H_2O(g) \\rightleftharpoons CO_2(g) + H_2(g)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$, where $\\Delta n$ counts gaseous moles only. $K_p = K_c$ only when $\\Delta n = 0$.\n- HI formation: $\\Delta n = 0$, so $K_p = K_c$.\n- NO formation: $\\Delta n = 0$, so $K_p = K_c$.\n- Water-gas shift: $\\Delta n = 0$, so $K_p = K_c$.\n- $PCl_5$ dissociation: $\\Delta n = +1$.\n- $SO_3$ formation: $\\Delta n = -1$.\n- $C + CO_2$: $\\Delta n = +1$, because the solid is not counted.\nThat gives 3 reactions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A reaction with a very large equilibrium constant may still take a very long time to reach equilibrium.\nReason (R): The equilibrium constant is related to the standard Gibbs energy change of the reaction and gives no information about the rate of the reaction.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. For example, $2SO_2 + O_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3$ has $K_c = 1.7 \\times 10^{26}$, yet the reaction is very slow without a catalyst.\nR is true. K depends on thermodynamics through $\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$, not on the rate.\nR explains why a large K does not guarantee a fast reaction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (relation between $K_p$ and $K_c$):\nList-I\n(A) $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$\n(B) $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$\n(C) $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$\n(D) $2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$\nList-II\n(i) $K_p = K_c(RT)$\n(ii) $K_p = K_c(RT)^{-1}$\n(iii) $K_p = K_c$\n(iv) $K_p = K_c(RT)^{-2}$","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $\\Delta n$ = gaseous product moles − gaseous reactant moles.\n(A) $\\Delta n = 2 - 2 = 0$, so $K_p = K_c$ → (iii).\n(B) $\\Delta n = 2 - 1 = +1$ → (i).\n(C) $\\Delta n = 2 - 4 = -2$ → (iv).\n(D) $\\Delta n = 2 - 3 = -1$ → (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For H$_2$(g) + I$_2$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2HI(g), the same equilibrium mixture is obtained whether we start with pure H$_2$ and I$_2$ or with pure HI, provided the total numbers of H and I atoms in the given volume are the same.\nReason (R): Once equilibrium is reached, both the forward and the reverse reactions stop completely.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT (Fig. 6.5) shows that this equilibrium can be reached from either direction. With equal totals of H and I atoms, the final composition is identical.\nR is false. Equilibrium is dynamic: both reactions continue at equal rates, so there is no net change in composition.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"When sodium chloride dissolves in water, the electrostatic attraction between $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ ions becomes much weaker than in the solid. By roughly what factor is this attraction reduced?","image":"","options":["18","55.5","80","$1.8 \\times 10^{9}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Electrostatic force between two charges is inversely proportional to the dielectric constant of the medium.\nWater has a very high dielectric constant of about 80.\nSo the attraction between $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ falls to about 1/80 of its value in vacuum. Hydration also keeps the ions well separated.\n18 is the molar mass of water and 55.5 M is the molarity of pure water, so those options are unrelated numbers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $Q_c < K_c$\n(b) $Q_c > K_c$\n(c) $Q_c = K_c$\n(d) $K_c > 10^3$\nList-II\n(i) No net reaction occurs\n(ii) Net reaction goes from right to left\n(iii) Products predominate at equilibrium\n(iv) Net reaction goes from left to right","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) When $Q_c < K_c$, there are too few products, so the net reaction goes forward: (iv).\n(b) When $Q_c > K_c$, there are too many products, so the net reaction goes in reverse: (ii).\n(c) When $Q_c = K_c$, the mixture is at equilibrium and no net reaction occurs: (i).\n(d) When $K_c > 10^3$, products predominate and the reaction goes nearly to completion: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the equilibrium between the pink aqua complex of cobalt and the blue complex: pink $[Co(H_2O)_6]^{n+}(aq) + 4Cl^-(aq) \\rightleftharpoons [CoCl_4]^{2-}(aq)$ (blue) $+ 6H_2O(l)$. At room temperature the mixture is blue.\nStatement I: When this mixture is placed in a freezing mixture, its blue colour becomes deeper.\nStatement II: The conversion of the pink aqua complex into blue $[CoCl_4]^{2-}$ is endothermic.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT observes that on cooling in a freezing mixture, the mixture turns pink, not deeper blue. So Statement I is incorrect.\nCooling favours the exothermic direction. Since cooling produces the pink complex, the reverse reaction (blue to pink) is exothermic.\nTherefore the forward reaction (pink to blue) is endothermic, and its K decreases on cooling. Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$, $K_c = 0.105$ at 773 K. Calculate $K_p$ at this temperature. (Take $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["$2.54 \\times 10^{-5}$","6.75","$1.63 \\times 10^{-3}$","$4.33 \\times 10^{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_c = 0.105$, T = 773 K, $\\Delta n = 2 - 4 = -2$.\nFormula: $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$.\nRT = 0.0831 × 773 = 64.24, so $(RT)^2$ = 4126.\nSubstituting: $K_p = \\frac{0.105}{4126} = 2.54 \\times 10^{-5}$.\nThe value 433 comes from multiplying by $(RT)^2$ instead of dividing.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (conclusion at equilibrium).\nList-I:\n(A) H$_2$O(l) ⇌ H$_2$O(g)\n(B) H$_2$O(s) ⇌ H$_2$O(l)\n(C) Sugar(s) ⇌ Sugar(solution)\n(D) CO$_2$(g) ⇌ CO$_2$(aq)\nList-II:\n(I) Melting point is fixed at constant pressure\n(II) [CO$_2$(aq)]/[CO$_2$(g)] is constant at a given temperature\n(III) $p_{H_2O}$ is constant at a given temperature\n(IV) Concentration of solute in solution is constant at a given temperature","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: For liquid ⇌ vapour, the vapour pressure is constant at a given temperature, so A matches (III).\nB: For solid ⇌ liquid, the melting point is fixed at constant pressure, so B matches (I).\nC: For a dissolving solid, the solubility is constant at a given temperature, so C matches (IV).\nD: For a gas dissolving in a liquid, the ratio [gas(aq)]/[gas(g)] is constant at a given temperature, so D matches (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A watch glass holding 1 mL of water, left open to the atmosphere, eventually loses all its water.\nStatement II: In an open system the vapour molecules spread into a large volume, so the rate of condensation stays far below the rate of evaporation and equilibrium cannot be reached.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct, and Statement II correctly explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT notes that liquids in open watch glasses eventually disappear.\nStatement II is correct. Evaporation continues at a steady rate, but the vapour disperses into the room. Condensation back into the liquid therefore stays much slower than evaporation.\nEquilibrium needs a closed system, where the two rates can become equal. Statement II is therefore the reason for Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although $K_c$ for $2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$ is about $1.7 \\times 10^{26}$, a catalyst such as $V_2O_5$ is used in the contact process.\nReason (R): The magnitude of an equilibrium constant tells us about the extent of a reaction but gives no information about the rate at which equilibrium is reached.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The huge $K_c$ means the reaction would go nearly to completion. In practice, however, oxidation of $SO_2$ is very slow, so Pt or $V_2O_5$ is used to speed it up.\nR is true. NCERT states that K says nothing about how fast equilibrium is attained.\nR explains A. Because a large K does not guarantee a fast reaction, a catalyst is still needed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, $K_a$ of HF is $3.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ and $K_a$ of HCN is $4.9 \\times 10^{-10}$. For the equilibrium\n$HF + CN^- \\rightleftharpoons F^- + HCN$\nwhich statement is correct?","image":"","options":["The equilibrium lies to the left because HCN is a stronger acid than HF","The equilibrium lies to the left because $F^-$ is a stronger base than $CN^-$","Neither direction is favoured since both HF and HCN are weak acids","The equilibrium lies to the right because HF is the stronger acid and $CN^-$ the stronger base, forming the weaker acid HCN and the weaker base $F^-$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A larger $K_a$ means a stronger acid, so HF ($3.5 \\times 10^{-4}$) is far stronger than HCN ($4.9 \\times 10^{-10}$).\nA weaker acid has a stronger conjugate base, so $CN^-$ is a stronger base than $F^-$.\nThe stronger acid donates a proton to the stronger base. The equilibrium therefore moves toward the weaker acid (HCN) and the weaker base ($F^-$).\nOption 3 fails because the two acids differ in strength by about $10^6$ times.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about equilibria in physical processes are correct?\n(i) Equilibrium is possible only in a closed system at a given temperature.\n(ii) At equilibrium, both opposing processes stop.\n(iii) All measurable properties of the system remain constant at equilibrium.\n(iv) At 1 atm, solid and liquid phases of a pure substance can coexist over a range of temperatures.\n(v) For gases dissolving in liquids, the concentration of the gas in the liquid is proportional to the pressure of the gas over the liquid.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: an open system cannot reach equilibrium.\n(ii) Incorrect: the equilibrium is dynamic, and both processes continue at equal rates.\n(iii) Correct: all measurable properties remain constant.\n(iv) Incorrect: at 1 atm there is only one temperature, the melting point, at which the two phases coexist.\n(v) Correct: this is Henry's law.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At a certain temperature, an equilibrium mixture for N$_2$O$_4$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2NO$_2$(g) contains [N$_2$O$_4$] = 0.040 M and [NO$_2$] = 0.012 M. What is the value of $K_c$ for the reaction 2NO$_2$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ N$_2$O$_4$(g) at the same temperature?","image":"","options":["$3.6 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol$^{-1}$ L","$2.78 \\times 10^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$ L","16.7 mol$^{-1}$ L","0.060 mol$^{-1}$ L"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: [N$_2$O$_4$] = 0.040 M and [NO$_2$] = 0.012 M.\nFormula: for 2NO$_2$ $\\rightleftharpoons$ N$_2$O$_4$, $K_c = \\frac{[N_2O_4]}{[NO_2]^2}$. This is the reciprocal of $K_c$ for the forward dissociation.\nForward: $K_c = \\frac{(0.012)^2}{0.040} = 3.6 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol L$^{-1}$.\nReverse: $K'_c = \\frac{1}{3.6 \\times 10^{-3}} = 277.8$ mol$^{-1}$ L $\\approx 2.78 \\times 10^2$ mol$^{-1}$ L.\nOption (1) is the forward constant, not the one asked.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the equilibrium $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$, $\\Delta H = -92.38$ kJ mol$^{-1}$, consider these changes made to the system at equilibrium:\n(i) adding more $N_2$ at constant volume\n(ii) halving the volume of the vessel at constant temperature\n(iii) raising the temperature\n(iv) adding an iron catalyst\n(v) adding argon at constant volume\n(vi) removing $NH_3$ continuously\nHow many of these change the numerical value of $K_c$?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i), (ii) and (vi) Do not change $K_c$. Changing concentration or pressure only changes Q, and the system shifts until Q equals the same $K_c$.\n(iv) Does not change $K_c$. A catalyst affects only the rate.\n(v) Does not change $K_c$. Argon at constant volume leaves the partial pressures unchanged.\n(iii) Changes $K_c$. For this exothermic reaction, K decreases as temperature rises.\nOnly one change alters $K_c$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the equilibrium used in the purification of nickel,\nNi(s) + 4CO(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ Ni(CO)$_4$(g),\nwhich expression correctly gives $K_c$?","image":"","options":["$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{Ni(CO)}_4]}{[\\text{Ni}][\\text{CO}]^4}$","$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{Ni(CO)}_4]}{[\\text{CO}]^4}$","$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{Ni(CO)}_4]}{4[\\text{CO}]}$","$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{CO}]^4}{[\\text{Ni(CO)}_4]}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ni is a pure solid. Its molar concentration is constant, so it is left out of the expression.\nCO is raised to its stoichiometric coefficient, 4, as a power and not as a multiplier.\nSo $K_c = [\\text{Ni(CO)}_4]/[\\text{CO}]^4$.\nOption (1) wrongly includes the solid. Option (4) is the expression for the reverse reaction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$, $K_p = \\frac{4}{3}$ bar at a certain temperature. Pure $N_2O_4$ is allowed to dissociate until the total equilibrium pressure is 1.0 bar. What is the degree of dissociation $\\alpha$ of $N_2O_4$?","image":"","options":["0.25","0.33","0.50","0.67"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: start with 1 mol $N_2O_4$. At equilibrium there are $(1-\\alpha)$ mol $N_2O_4$ and $2\\alpha$ mol $NO_2$, so total moles = $(1+\\alpha)$ and P = 1.0 bar.\nFormula: $p_{NO_2} = \\frac{2\\alpha}{1+\\alpha}P$ and $p_{N_2O_4} = \\frac{1-\\alpha}{1+\\alpha}P$, so $K_p = \\frac{4\\alpha^2 P}{1-\\alpha^2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{4\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha^2} = \\frac{4}{3}$, so $3\\alpha^2 = 1 - \\alpha^2$, giving $\\alpha^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$.\nSo $\\alpha = 0.50$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $CO_2(g) + C(s) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)$, $K_p = 3.0$ at 1000 K. Pure $CO_2$ at 1.00 bar is placed with excess graphite, and initially $p_{CO} = 0$. What is the partial pressure of CO at equilibrium? ($\\sqrt{57} = 7.55$)","image":"","options":["1.14 bar","0.57 bar","0.43 bar","1.73 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Set up: let $p_{CO_2}$ fall by x. At equilibrium $p_{CO_2} = 1.00 - x$ and $p_{CO} = 2x$. The pure solid C does not appear in $K_p$.\nFormula: $K_p = \\frac{p_{CO}^2}{p_{CO_2}}$, so $\\frac{(2x)^2}{1 - x} = 3.0$.\nRearrange: $4x^2 + 3x - 3 = 0$.\nSolve: $x = \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{57}}{8} = 0.569$, taking the positive root.\nSo $p_{CO} = 2x = 1.14$ bar and $p_{CO_2} = 0.43$ bar.\nThe option 0.43 bar is the $CO_2$ pressure, not CO.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In the NCERT activity, coloured water is transferred between two measuring cylinders using glass tubes. After the levels in both cylinders become constant, the students keep transferring water. What does this continued transfer at constant levels represent?","image":"","options":["The reaction has stopped completely at equilibrium.","The forward and reverse processes continue at equal rates, so there is no net change.","Only the forward process continues after equilibrium.","The equilibrium constant keeps increasing with time."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In this activity, the 'level' of water stands for the concentration of reactants and products.\nThe transfer in both directions continues, yet the levels do not change.\nThis means the two opposing processes occur at the same rate, which is the essence of dynamic equilibrium.\nOption (1) describes a static equilibrium, which contradicts the ongoing transfer.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Solution P has pH 3 and solution Q has pH 5 at the same temperature. The hydrogen ion concentration of P is how many times that of Q?","image":"","options":["20 times","2 times","1000 times","100 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: pH(P) = 3 and pH(Q) = 5.\nFormula: $[H^+] = 10^{-pH}$.\nSubstituting: $[H^+]_P = 10^{-3}$ M and $[H^+]_Q = 10^{-5}$ M.\nRatio $= \\frac{10^{-3}}{10^{-5}} = 100$.\nThe pH scale is logarithmic, so each unit of pH means a factor of 10 in $[H^+]$. Answering 2 times is the common mistake of treating pH as linear.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the methanation reaction $CO(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons CH_4(g) + H_2O(g)$, $K_c = 3.92$ (concentrations in mol $L^{-1}$) at a certain temperature. The equilibrium mixture, held in a cylinder fitted with a piston, is suddenly compressed to half its volume at constant temperature. What is the value of the reaction quotient $Q_c$ (in the same units) immediately after compression?","image":"","options":["0.98","3.92","15.68","1.96"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_c = 3.92$. Halving the volume doubles every concentration.\nFormula: $Q_c = \\frac{[CH_4][H_2O]}{[CO][H_2]^3}$.\nSubstituting doubled values: $Q_c = \\frac{(2)(2)}{(2)(2^3)}K_c = \\frac{K_c}{4} = \\frac{3.92}{4}$.\nSo $Q_c = 0.98$. Since $Q_c < K_c$, the reaction then proceeds forward.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$, $K_c = 57.0$ at 700 K. At some instant, a mixture at 700 K contains $[H_2] = 0.050$ M, $[I_2] = 0.050$ M and $[HI] = 0.50$ M. What will happen as the system moves towards equilibrium?","image":"","options":["Net reaction proceeds in the forward direction because $Q_c < K_c$","Net reaction proceeds in the reverse direction because $Q_c > K_c$","No net reaction occurs because the mixture is already at equilibrium","Net reaction proceeds in the forward direction because $Q_c > K_c$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: $Q_c = \\frac{[HI]^2}{[H_2][I_2]}$.\nSubstituting: $Q_c = \\frac{(0.50)^2}{(0.050)(0.050)} = \\frac{0.25}{0.0025} = 100$.\nCompare: $Q_c = 100$ is greater than $K_c = 57.0$.\nWhen $Q_c > K_c$, net reaction goes from right to left, so some HI decomposes into $H_2$ and $I_2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"2.0 mol of $PCl_5$ is placed in a 1.0 L closed vessel at a temperature where $K_c = 0.50$ for $PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$. What is the degree of dissociation of $PCl_5$ at equilibrium? (Solve exactly; do not assume $\\alpha \\ll 1$.)","image":"","options":["0.25","0.39","0.50","0.78"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: c = 2.0 M, $K_c$ = 0.50.\nICE table: at equilibrium $[PCl_5] = 2.0 - x$ and $[PCl_3] = [Cl_2] = x$.\nFormula: $\\frac{x^2}{2.0 - x} = 0.50$, which gives $x^2 + 0.50x - 1.0 = 0$.\nSubstituting into the quadratic formula: $x = \\frac{-0.50 + \\sqrt{0.25 + 4.0}}{2} = \\frac{-0.50 + 2.062}{2} = 0.781$ M.\nSo $\\alpha = \\frac{x}{c} = \\frac{0.781}{2.0} \\approx 0.39$.\nThe approximation $\\sqrt{K_c/c}$ gives 0.50, which is wrong here because α is not small. The value 0.78 is x itself, not α.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In the NCERT experiment to measure the equilibrium vapour pressure of water, anhydrous calcium chloride (or phosphorus pentoxide) is kept in the closed box for a few hours. It is removed before the watch glass of water is placed inside. The purpose of this drying agent is to:","image":"","options":["Increase the rate of evaporation of water","Remove water vapour so that the box initially contains almost no water vapour","Absorb heat so that the temperature stays constant","React with air so that only water vapour remains in the box"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The drying agent absorbs moisture from the air inside the box.\nAs a result, there is no (or very little) water vapour in the box at the start.\nAny rise in manometer pressure can then be attributed entirely to water evaporating from the watch glass.\nThe drying agent does not control temperature, and it does not remove air.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$, the value of $K_c$ at 500 K is $3.55 \\times 10^{2}$ (concentrations in mol/L). What is the value of $K_p$ at the same temperature, with pressures in bar? (Take $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["$0.206$","$8.54$","$1.48 \\times 10^{4}$","$6.13 \\times 10^{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_c = 355$, $T = 500$ K, $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$, where $\\Delta n = 2 - (1+3) = -2$.\nSubstituting: $RT = 0.0831 \\times 500 = 41.55$, so $(RT)^2 = 1726.4$.\n$K_p = 355 / 1726.4$.\nSo $K_p \\approx 0.206$.\nCommon slips give the other options: using $\\Delta n = -1$ gives 8.54, and using $\\Delta n = +1$ gives $1.48 \\times 10^4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The solubility of AgCl in an aqueous NaCl solution is lower than its solubility in pure water.\nReason (R): The solubility product of AgCl decreases when NaCl is added to its saturated solution.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NaCl supplies $Cl^-$, a common ion. This raises the ionic product $[Ag^+][Cl^-]$ above $K_{sp}$. AgCl therefore precipitates until the product again equals $K_{sp}$, so less AgCl stays dissolved (common ion effect).\nR is false. $K_{sp}$ is an equilibrium constant. It depends only on temperature, not on the concentrations of the ions present.\nThe solubility falls because the $Cl^-$ concentration rises while $K_{sp}$ stays fixed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Pure $N_2O_4$ is placed in a sealed rigid vessel at 400 K, where its initial pressure is 2.0 bar. It dissociates as $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$. At equilibrium the total pressure is 2.6 bar at the same temperature. What is $K_p$ for the reaction?","image":"","options":["1.03 bar","0.514 bar","0.257 bar","0.72 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: initial $p_{N_2O_4}$ = 2.0 bar, $p_{total}$ = 2.6 bar.\nLet x be the drop in $p_{N_2O_4}$. At equilibrium, $p_{N_2O_4} = 2.0 - x$ and $p_{NO_2} = 2x$.\nTotal pressure: $2.0 + x = 2.6$, so $x = 0.6$ bar.\nThis gives $p_{NO_2}$ = 1.2 bar and $p_{N_2O_4}$ = 1.4 bar.\nFormula: $K_p = \\frac{(p_{NO_2})^2}{p_{N_2O_4}}$\nSubstituting: $K_p = \\frac{(1.2)^2}{1.4} = \\frac{1.44}{1.4} \\approx 1.03$ bar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In the equilibrium $Fe^{3+}(aq) + SCN^-(aq) \\rightleftharpoons [Fe(SCN)]^{2+}(aq)$, the solution is deep red because of $[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$. A few drops of aqueous $HgCl_2$ are added to the equilibrium mixture. What is observed, and why?","image":"","options":["The red colour deepens because $Hg^{2+}$ forms a complex with $Fe^{3+}$ and releases more $SCN^-$","The red colour fades because $Hg^{2+}$ removes free $SCN^-$ as $[Hg(SCN)_4]^{2-}$, shifting the equilibrium to the left","The red colour fades because $Hg^{2+}$ removes free $Fe^{3+}$ as $[Fe(C_2O_4)_3]^{3-}$","There is no change in colour because $HgCl_2$ does not take part in the equilibrium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: $Hg^{2+}$ reacts with $SCN^-$ to form the stable complex ion $[Hg(SCN)_4]^{2-}$.\nThis lowers the concentration of free $SCN^-$, a reactant.\nBy Le Chatelier's principle, $[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$ dissociates to replace the lost $SCN^-$, so the equilibrium shifts from right to left and the red colour fades.\nOption (3) describes the effect of oxalic acid, not $HgCl_2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At a certain temperature above 298 K, $K_w = 4.0\\times10^{-14}$. Take $\\log 2 = 0.30$.\nStatement I: At this temperature, pure water has a pH of about 6.70, yet it is still neutral.\nStatement II: At this temperature, a 0.01 M NaOH solution has a pH of 12.00.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: In pure water, $[H^+] = [OH^-] = \\sqrt{4.0\\times10^{-14}} = 2\\times10^{-7}$ M.\nSo pH $= 7 - 0.30 = 6.70$. Since $[H^+] = [OH^-]$, the water is neutral. Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: $pK_w = 14 - 0.60 = 13.40$. For 0.01 M NaOH, pOH = 2.\nSo pH $= 13.40 - 2 = 11.40$, not 12.00. The value 12.00 wrongly uses $pK_w = 14$. Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.10 M solution of a weak monobasic acid HA has pH = 3.00 at 298 K. This solution is diluted to a concentration of $1.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ M. Find the pH of the diluted solution. Solve exactly, without assuming $1-\\alpha \\approx 1$, and neglect the ionisation of water. (log 2.71 = 0.433)","image":"","options":["4.57","4.50","5.00","3.57"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find $K_a$. At pH 3.00, [H$^+$] = $10^{-3}$ M, so $K_a = \\frac{(10^{-3})^2}{0.10 - 0.001} = 1.01 \\times 10^{-5}$.\nStep 2: Set up the diluted solution with $c = 10^{-4}$ M: $\\frac{x^2}{c - x} = K_a$, which gives $x^2 + K_a x - K_a c = 0$.\nStep 3: Solve the quadratic: $x = \\frac{-1.01\\times10^{-5} + \\sqrt{(1.01\\times10^{-5})^2 + 4.04\\times10^{-9}}}{2} = 2.71 \\times 10^{-5}$ M.\nStep 4: Calculate pH: pH = $5 - 0.433 = 4.57$.\nThe degree of ionisation is about 0.27, so the approximation (which gives 4.50) is invalid here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The separation of ions when NaCl dissolves in water is strictly called dissociation, whereas the formation of ions from HCl in water is called ionization.\nReason (R): NaCl already exists as ions in its solid state, while HCl is a polar covalent molecule that splits into ions only in solution.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Dissociation means separating ions that already exist in the solid solute. Ionization means a neutral molecule splitting into ions in solution.\nR is true. Solid NaCl is a cluster of $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ ions, while HCl is a polar covalent molecule.\nR gives exactly the basis of the distinction stated in A, so it is the correct explanation.\nNCERT notes that the two terms are often used interchangeably.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The degree of ionization of 0.10 M acetic acid ($K_a = 1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$) is first found in pure water. It is then found in a solution that is 0.10 M in acetic acid and also 0.10 M in HCl. By approximately what factor does the degree of ionization of acetic acid decrease in the presence of HCl? (Assume $\\alpha \\ll 1$ in both cases and that HCl is fully ionized.)","image":"","options":["7.45","74.5","1.34","745"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $c = 0.10$ M and $K_a = 1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$.\nIn water: $\\alpha_1 = \\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{c}} = \\sqrt{1.8 \\times 10^{-4}} = 1.34 \\times 10^{-2}$.\nWith HCl: $[H^+] \\approx 0.10$ M, which comes almost entirely from HCl. Then $K_a = \\frac{(0.10)(c\\alpha_2)}{c}$, so $\\alpha_2 = \\frac{K_a}{0.10} = 1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nFactor: $\\frac{\\alpha_1}{\\alpha_2} = \\frac{1.34 \\times 10^{-2}}{1.8 \\times 10^{-4}} \\approx 74.5$.\nSo the ionization falls about 74.5 times because of the common $H^+$ ion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The molar concentration of a pure solid taking part in an equilibrium is constant, whatever amount of the solid is present.\nStatement II: The molar concentration of a substance in aqueous solution, [X(aq)], also stays constant whatever amount of X is dissolved in a given volume.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For a pure solid or liquid, the amount per unit volume is fixed by its density, so [X(s)] and [X(l)] are constant.\nStatement II is incorrect. [X(aq)] and [X(g)] change as the amount of X in a given volume changes.\nThat is why aqueous and gaseous species appear in K expressions, while pure solids and liquids do not.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In the large-scale production of quicklime, $CaCO_3(s) \\rightleftharpoons CaO(s) + CO_2(g)$, carbon dioxide is continuously removed from the kiln. This drives the decomposition towards completion because:","image":"","options":["removal of $CO_2$ keeps the reaction quotient below the equilibrium constant, so net reaction keeps going forward","removal of $CO_2$ increases the value of the equilibrium constant","removal of $CO_2$ lowers the temperature of the kiln","removal of $CO_2$ lowers the concentrations of $CaO$ and $CaCO_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For this reaction, $K_p = p_{CO_2}$, since the pure solids do not appear in the expression.\nRemoving $CO_2$ makes $Q < K$. The system replenishes the removed product by decomposing more $CaCO_3$.\nContinuous removal keeps Q below K, so the reaction never stops short of completion.\nK depends only on temperature, so option (2) is wrong.\nThe concentrations of pure solids are constant, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $CO_2(g) + C(s) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)$, $K_p = 3.0$ at 1000 K. A vessel containing excess graphite at 1000 K initially has $p_{CO_2} = 0.50$ bar and $p_{CO} = 1.50$ bar. What is the partial pressure of CO when equilibrium is re-established? (Take $\\sqrt{69} = 8.31$.)","image":"","options":["1.17 bar","1.33 bar","1.50 bar","1.62 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $Q_p = \\frac{(1.50)^2}{0.50} = 4.5$, which is greater than 3.0, so the net reaction goes in reverse.\nLet $p_{CO_2}$ rise by y. Then $p_{CO} = 1.50 - 2y$ and $p_{CO_2} = 0.50 + y$.\nFormula: $\\frac{(1.50-2y)^2}{0.50+y} = 3.0$, which simplifies to $4y^2 - 9y + 0.75 = 0$.\nSubstituting: $y = \\frac{9 - \\sqrt{69}}{8} = \\frac{9 - 8.31}{8} = 0.087$. The other root (about 2.2) is rejected because it would make $p_{CO}$ negative.\nSo $p_{CO} = 1.50 - 0.17 = 1.33$ bar.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Of two liquids at the same temperature, the one with the higher equilibrium vapour pressure is more volatile and has a lower boiling point.\nStatement II: The boiling point of water at a hill station at high altitude is higher than 100°C because the atmospheric pressure there is lower.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that a liquid with a higher vapour pressure is more volatile and has a lower boiling point.\nStatement II is incorrect: the boiling point depends on atmospheric pressure. At high altitude the pressure is lower, so the boiling point decreases below 100°C rather than increasing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Once chemical equilibrium is reached in a closed vessel, the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant with time.\nReason (R): At equilibrium, both the forward and the reverse reactions stop.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: at chemical equilibrium, the concentrations of all species stay constant.\nR is false: neither reaction stops at equilibrium. The forward and reverse rates simply become equal, which makes the equilibrium dynamic.\nThe isotope (deuterium) scrambling experiment confirms that both reactions continue at equilibrium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.20 M aqueous solution of a weak monoacidic base B has pH = 11.0 at 298 K. What is the $pK_a$ of its conjugate acid $BH^+$? ($K_w = 1.0 \\times 10^{-14}$; $\\log 5.03 = 0.70$)","image":"","options":["5.30","9.70","8.70","7.30"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: pH = 11.0, so pOH = 3.0 and $[OH^-] = [BH^+] = 10^{-3}$ M.\nFormula: $K_b = \\frac{[BH^+][OH^-]}{[B]}$ and $pK_a + pK_b = 14$.\nSubstituting: $K_b = \\frac{(10^{-3})^2}{0.20 - 0.001} = 5.03 \\times 10^{-6}$, so $pK_b = 6 - 0.70 = 5.30$.\nSo $pK_a = 14 - 5.30 = 8.70$. Option (1) is $pK_b$, not $pK_a$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The equilibrium $PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$ is established in a vessel at a fixed temperature. Argon gas, which does not react, is then introduced. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["If argon is added at constant volume, the total pressure rises and the equilibrium shifts backward","If argon is added at constant total pressure, the volume increases, $Q_p$ becomes less than $K_p$, and more $PCl_5$ dissociates","If argon is added at constant total pressure, $K_p$ increases, so more $PCl_5$ dissociates","If argon is added at constant total pressure, the equilibrium shifts backward because the total number of moles in the vessel increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: at constant volume, the partial pressures of the reacting gases are unchanged, so there is no shift.\n(2) Correct: at constant total pressure, the volume must increase. Each partial pressure falls by the same factor $f < 1$, so $Q_p = fK_p < K_p$. The reaction moves forward, towards more gas moles.\n(3) Incorrect: $K_p$ depends only on temperature.\n(4) Incorrect: the argon moles do not appear in $Q_p$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For a homogeneous gaseous reaction at 300 K, the numerical ratio $\\frac{K_p}{K_c}$ (pressures in bar, concentrations in mol/L) is found to be $1.61 \\times 10^{-3}$. Which reaction could it be? (Take $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["$N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$","$2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$","$PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$","$2NH_3(g) \\rightleftharpoons N_2(g) + 3H_2(g)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"From $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$, the ratio $\\frac{K_p}{K_c} = (RT)^{\\Delta n}$.\nRT = 0.0831 × 300 = 24.93, and $(24.93)^2$ = 621.5.\nSo $(RT)^{-2} = \\frac{1}{621.5} = 1.61 \\times 10^{-3}$, giving $\\Delta n = -2$.\n$NH_3$ synthesis: $\\Delta n = 2 - 4 = -2$, which fits.\n$SO_3$ formation has $\\Delta n = -1$ (ratio 0.040). $PCl_5$ dissociation has $\\Delta n = +1$. $NH_3$ decomposition has $\\Delta n = +2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For CO$_2$(g) + C(s) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2CO(g), $K_p = 3.0$ at 1000 K. Calculate $K_c$ at this temperature. Take R = 0.0831 bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["$3.61 \\times 10^{-2}$ mol L$^{-1}$","249 mol L$^{-1}$","3.0 mol L$^{-1}$","$4.34 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol L$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_p = 3.0$, T = 1000 K, R = 0.0831 bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$. Count only gaseous species: $\\Delta n$ = 2 − 1 = 1, since C(s) is excluded.\nSubstituting: $K_c = 3.0/(0.0831 \\times 1000) = 3.0/83.1$.\nSo $K_c = 3.61 \\times 10^{-2}$ mol L$^{-1}$.\nOption (3) results from wrongly counting C as a gas, which gives $\\Delta n$ = 0.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At a certain temperature, $K_p = 4.0$ for CO$_2$(g) + C(s) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2CO(g). CO$_2$ at an initial pressure of 1.00 bar is introduced into a closed vessel containing excess graphite, with no CO present initially. What is the equilibrium partial pressure of CO? (Take $\\sqrt{5} = 2.236$.)","image":"","options":["0.62 bar","0.38 bar","1.24 bar","2.00 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let x bar of CO$_2$ react. At equilibrium, $p_{CO_2}$ = (1 − x) and $p_{CO}$ = 2x.\nThe solid is omitted, so $K_p = (2x)^2/(1-x) = 4.0$.\nThis gives $4x^2 = 4 - 4x$, i.e. $x^2 + x - 1 = 0$.\nSolving: $x = (-1 + \\sqrt{5})/2 = 0.618$. The negative root is rejected.\nSo $p_{CO}$ = 2x = 1.24 bar. Option (1) gives x itself, not 2x.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 500 K, for which of the following gaseous equilibria will $K_p$ be numerically greater than $K_c$? (Take $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and pressures in bar.)","image":"","options":["$N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$","$H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$","$PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$","$2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The relation is $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$. At 500 K, $RT = 0.0831 \\times 500 = 41.6$, which is greater than 1.\nSo $K_p > K_c$ only when $\\Delta n$ is positive.\n$NH_3$ synthesis: $\\Delta n = 2 - 4 = -2$, so $K_p < K_c$.\nHI formation: $\\Delta n = 0$, so $K_p = K_c$.\n$PCl_5$ dissociation: $\\Delta n = 2 - 1 = +1$, so $K_p > K_c$.\n$SO_3$ formation: $\\Delta n = 2 - 3 = -1$, so $K_p < K_c$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 500 K (so that RT > 1 with R in bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$), consider the following homogeneous gaseous equilibria. How many of them have $K_p > K_c$?\n(i) $PCl_5 \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3 + Cl_2$\n(ii) $2NOCl \\rightleftharpoons 2NO + Cl_2$\n(iii) $N_2O_4 \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2$\n(iv) $H_2 + I_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2HI$\n(v) $N_2 + 3H_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3$\n(vi) $2SO_2 + O_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3$\n(vii) $CO + 3H_2 \\rightleftharpoons CH_4 + H_2O$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Since RT = 41.55 > 1, $K_p > K_c$ only when $\\Delta n > 0$.\n(i) $\\Delta n = +1$, (ii) $\\Delta n = +1$, (iii) $\\Delta n = +1$: all give $K_p > K_c$.\n(iv) $\\Delta n = 0$, so $K_p = K_c$.\n(v) $\\Delta n = -2$, (vi) $\\Delta n = -1$, (vii) $\\Delta n = -2$: all give $K_p < K_c$.\nCount = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Ice and water are kept in a perfectly insulated thermos flask at atmospheric pressure. The masses of ice and water do not change with time. For a pure substance at atmospheric pressure, the temperature at which the solid and liquid phases are in equilibrium is called its:","image":"","options":["Normal boiling point","Normal melting point","Sublimation temperature","Critical temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT definition: for any pure substance at atmospheric pressure, the temperature at which solid and liquid coexist in equilibrium is its normal melting point (or normal freezing point). For water this is 273 K.\nThe normal boiling point refers to liquid–vapour equilibrium, not solid–liquid equilibrium, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Two sealed tubes containing the equilibrium $2NO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons N_2O_4(g)$, $\\Delta H = -57.2\\ kJ\\,mol^{-1}$, with equal brown intensity are placed in a freezing mixture and in hot water (363 K) respectively.\nHow many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The brown colour fades in the tube kept in the freezing mixture.\n(ii) $K_c$ for the formation of $N_2O_4$ is larger at 363 K than at room temperature.\n(iii) Unlike a change in concentration, a change in temperature alters the value of $K_c$.\n(iv) The brown colour deepens in hot water because the reverse reaction is favoured.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the forward reaction is exothermic, so cooling favours colourless $N_2O_4$.\n(ii) Incorrect: for an exothermic reaction, K decreases as temperature rises.\n(iii) Correct: concentration or pressure changes alter only Q, whereas a temperature change alters K itself.\n(iv) Correct: heating favours the endothermic reverse reaction, which produces brown $NO_2$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the pH of a $5.0 \\times 10^{-8}$ M solution of HCl at 298 K ($K_w = 1.0 \\times 10^{-14}$). You may use $\\sqrt{4.25} = 2.06$ and $\\log 1.28 = 0.107$.","image":"","options":["7.30","6.82","6.89","7.00"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $c_{HCl} = 5 \\times 10^{-8}$ M. Let x = $[OH^-]$, which also equals the $[H_3O^+]$ coming from water.\nFormula: $K_w = (5 \\times 10^{-8} + x)(x) = 10^{-14}$, which gives $x^2 + 5 \\times 10^{-8}x - 10^{-14} = 0$\nSubstituting: $x = \\frac{-5 \\times 10^{-8} + 2.06 \\times 10^{-7}}{2} = 7.8 \\times 10^{-8}$\nTotal $[H^+] = 5 \\times 10^{-8} + 7.8 \\times 10^{-8} = 1.28 \\times 10^{-7}$ M\nSo pH = 7 − 0.107 = 6.89\nWhy the distractors fail: 7.30 ignores the $H^+$ from water. 6.82 wrongly adds $10^{-7}$, ignoring that the added acid suppresses water's ionization.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At constant volume, adding argon to the equilibrium mixture $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$ does not change the amount of ammonia present.\nReason (R): At constant volume, adding an inert gas does not change the partial pressures or molar concentrations of the reacting species.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: argon takes no part in the reaction, so at fixed volume the equilibrium is undisturbed.\nR is true: the moles of each reactant and product and the volume are unchanged, so their concentrations and partial pressures stay the same.\nBecause the concentrations are unchanged, $Q_c$ still equals $K_c$ and no shift occurs. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) pH is defined as the negative logarithm (base 10) of the activity of hydrogen ion.\n(ii) Activity of hydrogen ion carries the unit mol $L^{-1}$.\n(iii) At 298 K, pH + pOH = 14 for any aqueous solution.\n(iv) A saturated solution of NaOH can have a pH of about 15.\n(v) The self-ionization equilibrium of water lies mainly towards $H_3O^+$ and $OH^-$ ions.","image":"","options":["4","2","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: pH $= -\\log a_{H^+}$.\n(ii) Incorrect: activity has no unit, since $a_{H^+} = [H^+]/\\text{mol } L^{-1}$.\n(iii) Correct: $pK_w = pH + pOH = 14$ at 298 K.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT lists a saturated NaOH solution at pH about 15.\n(v) Incorrect: only about 2 in $10^9$ water molecules are dissociated.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the equilibrium $PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$, adding argon at constant total pressure increases the degree of dissociation of $PCl_5$.\nReason (R): At constant pressure, adding an inert gas increases the volume, which lowers the partial pressures of the reacting gases. The equilibrium then shifts towards the side with more moles of gas.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: to keep the total pressure constant, the volume must increase. This dilutes all reacting gases and lowers their partial pressures, which acts like a drop in pressure.\nA is true: the forward reaction produces 2 mol of gas from 1 mol, so the equilibrium shifts forward and more $PCl_5$ dissociates.\nR is exactly why A happens.\nContrast: at constant volume, an inert gas changes no partial pressure, so there is no shift.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) $BF_3$\n(b) $HCO_3^-$\n(c) $Cl^-$\n(d) $H_9O_4^+$\nList-II:\n(i) Hydrated form of the hydronium ion\n(ii) Conjugate base much weaker than $H_2O$\n(iii) Electron-deficient Lewis acid having no proton\n(iv) Amphiprotic species","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $BF_3$ has no proton but accepts an electron pair, so it matches (iii).\n(b) $HCO_3^-$ can give $H_2CO_3$ by gaining a proton or $CO_3^{2-}$ by losing one, so it matches (iv).\n(c) $Cl^-$ is the conjugate base of the strong acid HCl and is a much weaker base than $H_2O$, so it matches (ii).\n(d) $H_9O_4^+$ is $H_3O^+$ further hydrated by water molecules, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following species can NOT act as a Lewis acid?","image":"","options":["$BF_3$","$AlCl_3$","$Mg^{2+}$","$NH_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A Lewis acid accepts an electron pair. Electron-deficient species and cations can do this.\n$BF_3$ and $AlCl_3$ are electron deficient, and $Mg^{2+}$ is a cation, so all three accept lone pairs.\n$NH_3$ has a lone pair on nitrogen, which it donates. It acts as a Lewis base, as in the reaction $BF_3 + :NH_3 \\rightarrow BF_3:NH_3$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Pure water has density 1000 g/L and molar mass 18.0 g/mol. At 298 K, $[H^+] = 1.0\\times10^{-7}$ M. What is the ratio of the molar concentration of dissociated water to that of undissociated water?","image":"","options":["$1.0\\times10^{-7}$","$3.6\\times10^{-9}$","$1.8\\times10^{-9}$","$1.8\\times10^{-16}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: density = 1000 g/L, M = 18.0 g/mol, $[H^+] = 10^{-7}$ M.\nMolarity of water: $\\frac{1000}{18.0} = 55.55$ M.\nEach dissociating $H_2O$ gives one $H^+$, so $[H_2O]_{dissociated} = 10^{-7}$ M.\nRatio $= \\frac{10^{-7}}{55.55} = 1.8\\times10^{-9}$, about 2 in $10^9$. The equilibrium therefore lies mainly towards undissociated water.\nThe option $3.6\\times10^{-9}$ wrongly counts both ions as dissociated water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Adding argon gas at constant volume to an equilibrium mixture of $N_2$, $H_2$ and $NH_3$ does not change the amount of $NH_3$ present.\nReason (R): An inert gas added at constant volume leaves the partial pressures and molar concentrations of the reacting species unchanged, so $Q_c$ remains equal to $K_c$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that adding an inert gas at constant volume leaves the equilibrium undisturbed.\nR is true. The moles and volume of $N_2$, $H_2$ and $NH_3$ stay the same, so their concentrations and partial pressures stay the same.\n$Q_c$ changes only if the added gas takes part in the reaction. Since $Q_c$ still equals $K_c$, there is no shift, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A reaction has an equilibrium constant $K = 1.0 \\times 10^{3}$ at 300 K. What is its standard Gibbs energy change $\\Delta G^\\circ$? (R = 8.314 J $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$, ln 10 = 2.303)","image":"","options":["−17.2 kJ $mol^{-1}$","+17.2 kJ $mol^{-1}$","−7.48 kJ $mol^{-1}$","−2.49 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: K = $10^3$, T = 300 K, R = 8.314 J $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$\nConvert the log: $\\ln K = 3 \\times 2.303 = 6.909$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta G^\\circ = -8.314 \\times 300 \\times 6.909 \\approx -17230$ J $mol^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta G^\\circ \\approx -17.2$ kJ $mol^{-1}$. It is negative, which matches K > 1.\nThe option −7.48 comes from using $\\log_{10}$ without multiplying by 2.303.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a solution of acetic acid is diluted with water at constant temperature, its degree of ionization increases.\nReason (R): For a weak acid with small $\\alpha$, $\\alpha \\approx \\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{c}}$, and $K_a$ stays constant at a fixed temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. From $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha} \\approx c\\alpha^2$, we get $\\alpha \\approx \\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{c}}$. $K_a$ depends only on temperature.\nA is true. Dilution lowers $c$, and since $K_a$ is fixed, $\\alpha$ must rise.\nFor example, a 100-fold dilution increases $\\alpha$ about 10 times.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the law of chemical equilibrium are correct?\n(i) It was proposed in 1864 by the Norwegian chemists Guldberg and Waage.\n(ii) It is also called the law of mass action because concentration was earlier called 'active mass'.\n(iii) The powers of concentration terms in $K_c$ are the stoichiometric coefficients of the balanced equation.\n(iv) For $H_2 + I_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2HI$, the ratio $\\frac{[HI]}{[H_2][I_2]}$ has the same value in all equilibrium experiments.\n(v) The value of $K_c$ at a given temperature depends on the initial concentrations taken.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Guldberg and Waage proposed the law in 1864.\n(ii) Correct: 'active mass' was the old term for concentration.\n(iii) Correct: the exponents are the stoichiometric coefficients.\n(iv) Incorrect: this ratio is far from constant across experiments. Only $\\frac{[HI]^2}{[H_2][I_2]}$ is constant.\n(v) Incorrect: $K_c$ does not depend on initial concentrations; it depends only on temperature.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At a certain temperature, $K_c$ for the reaction $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$ is 50. What is $K_c$ for $2HI(g) \\rightleftharpoons H_2(g) + I_2(g)$ at the same temperature?","image":"","options":["7.07","50","0.141","0.02"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $K_c$ (forward) = 50.\nFormula: for the reverse reaction, $K'_c = \\frac{1}{K_c}$.\nSubstituting: $K'_c = \\frac{1}{50}$.\nSo $K'_c$ = 0.02.\nThe value 7.07 ($\\sqrt{50}$) would apply only to the halved forward equation.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following sets contains only substances that NCERT cites as examples of solid ⇌ vapour equilibrium in a closed vessel?","image":"","options":["Iodine, camphor, sugar","Iodine, camphor, ammonium chloride","Camphor, sodium chloride, ammonium chloride","Iodine, sugar, sodium chloride"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT cites three substances that sublime and set up a solid ⇌ vapour equilibrium in a closed vessel: I$_2$ (which gives violet vapour), camphor and NH$_4$Cl.\nSugar and NaCl do not sublime. Sugar is used in NCERT as an example of a solid dissolving in a liquid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"What is the degree of ionization of acetic acid in a solution that is 0.10 M in acetic acid and also 0.10 M in sodium acetate? ($K_a$ of acetic acid $= 1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$; assume sodium acetate is fully dissociated.)","image":"","options":["$1.34 \\times 10^{-2}$","$1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$","$4.2 \\times 10^{-3}$","$1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $[CH_3COOH] \\approx 0.10$ M; $[CH_3COO^-] \\approx 0.10$ M, which comes almost entirely from the salt (common ion).\nFormula: $K_a = \\frac{[H^+][A^-]}{[HA]}$, so $[H^+] = K_a\\frac{[HA]}{[A^-]} = 1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$ M.\nThen $\\alpha = \\frac{[H^+]}{c} = \\frac{1.8 \\times 10^{-5}}{0.10} = 1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nWithout the salt, $\\alpha = \\sqrt{K_a/c} = 1.34 \\times 10^{-2}$ (option 1). The common ion suppresses ionization.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 1069 K, the equilibrium $2NOCl(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO(g) + Cl_2(g)$ has $K_c = 3.75 \\times 10^{-6}$ (concentrations in mol/L). What is $K_p$ (pressures in bar) at the same temperature for the reaction $NO(g) + \\frac{1}{2}Cl_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons NOCl(g)$? (Take $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["$54.8$","$516$","$4.87 \\times 10^{3}$","$1.83 \\times 10^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_c = 3.75 \\times 10^{-6}$ for the forward reaction, and $RT = 0.0831 \\times 1069 = 88.83$.\nStep 1: The new reaction is the original one reversed and halved. So $K_c' = (1/K_c)^{1/2} = (2.667 \\times 10^5)^{1/2} = 516.4$.\nStep 2: For the new reaction, $\\Delta n = 1 - 1.5 = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\nStep 3: $K_p' = K_c'(RT)^{-1/2} = 516.4 / 9.425$.\nSo $K_p' \\approx 54.8$.\nThe value 516 comes from skipping the $(RT)$ conversion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A reaction with $\\Delta G^\\ominus = +5.7$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ at 298 K has an equilibrium constant of about 0.10 at that temperature. (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$; ln 10 = 2.303)\nStatement II: A positive $\\Delta G^\\ominus$ means that no product at all is present when such a reaction reaches equilibrium.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: $\\ln K = -\\frac{\\Delta G^\\ominus}{RT} = -\\frac{5700}{8.314 \\times 298} = -2.30$.\nSo $K = e^{-2.30} \\approx 0.10$. The statement is correct.\nStatement II: a positive $\\Delta G^\\ominus$ only makes K < 1. The forward reaction proceeds to a small extent, so some product is still present at equilibrium. K is never zero, so the statement is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An aqueous solution of acetic acid contains mainly unionized $CH_3COOH$ molecules, with only a small amount of acetate and hydronium ions.\nStatement II: The electrical conductance of an aqueous sodium chloride solution decreases as the concentration of NaCl increases.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Acetic acid is a weak electrolyte that ionizes to less than 5%. An equilibrium exists between its ions and the unionized molecules.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that conductance increases as the concentration of common salt increases, because more ions are available to carry charge.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"What is the pH of a $2.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ M aqueous HCl solution at 298 K, taking into account the ionization of water? ($K_w = 1.0 \\times 10^{-14}$; $\\sqrt{2} = 1.414$, log 2.414 = 0.383)","image":"","options":["6.62","6.70","7.00","6.38"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: [HCl] = $2 \\times 10^{-7}$ M. This is close to $10^{-7}$ M, so water's own $H^+$ cannot be ignored.\nSet up: let $x = [OH^-]$ from water. Then $[H^+] = 2\\times10^{-7} + x$ and $(2\\times10^{-7} + x)x = 10^{-14}$.\nSolve the quadratic: $x = \\frac{-2\\times10^{-7} + \\sqrt{8\\times10^{-14}}}{2} = 0.414\\times10^{-7}$.\nSo $[H^+] = 2.414\\times10^{-7}$ M, and pH = 7 − 0.383 = 6.62.\nThe option 6.70 ignores water's contribution.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For $2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$, $K_c = 1.7 \\times 10^{26}$, yet a catalyst such as $V_2O_5$ is used in the contact process.\nReason (R): The magnitude of the equilibrium constant gives no information about the rate at which equilibrium is reached.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the very large K shows the reaction can go almost to completion, but the oxidation of $SO_2$ is very slow in practice, so $V_2O_5$ or Pt is used as a catalyst.\nR is true: NCERT states that the equilibrium constant tells us the extent of a reaction, not its rate.\nR explains A: because a large K does not guarantee a fast reaction, a catalyst is still needed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the equilibrium $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$.\nStatement I: When concentrations are taken in mol/L and pressures in bar, $K_c$ has the unit mol/L and $K_p$ has the unit bar.\nStatement II: When the standard states are specified as 1 bar for gases and 1 M for solutes, $K_p$ and $K_c$ both become dimensionless and therefore have the same numerical value.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Here $K_c = [NO_2]^2/[N_2O_4]$, so its unit is (mol/L)$^2$/(mol/L) = mol/L. In the same way, $K_p$ has the unit bar.\nStatement II is incorrect. Dividing by the standard states (1 bar and 1 M) does make both constants dimensionless. However, their numerical values stay different because the standard states are different. They are still related by $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$, and here $\\Delta n = 1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Solid sodium acetate is dissolved in a 0.1 M acetic acid solution at constant temperature. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $[H_3O^+]$ of the solution decreases.\n(ii) The pH of the solution increases.\n(iii) The $K_a$ of acetic acid decreases.\n(iv) The degree of ionization of acetic acid decreases.\n(v) The total acetate ion concentration in the solution decreases.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sodium acetate supplies the common ion $CH_3COO^-$, which shifts the equilibrium $CH_3COOH \\rightleftharpoons H^+ + CH_3COO^-$ to the left.\n(i) Correct: $[H_3O^+]$ falls.\n(ii) Correct: lower $[H_3O^+]$ means higher pH.\n(iii) Incorrect: $K_a$ changes only with temperature.\n(iv) Correct: the common ion suppresses ionization, so $\\alpha$ falls.\n(v) Incorrect: total acetate rises because of the added salt.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) If $\\Delta G^\\circ = 0$ for a reaction, its equilibrium constant equals 1.\n(ii) If $Q > K$ for a reaction mixture, $\\Delta G$ for the forward reaction is positive.\n(iii) A catalyst lowers $\\Delta G^\\circ$ of a reaction and therefore increases K.\n(iv) A reaction with $K < 1$ cannot proceed in the forward direction at all.\n(v) At equilibrium, $\\Delta G^\\circ$ of the reaction becomes zero.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $K = e^{0} = 1$.\n(ii) Correct: $\\Delta G = RT\\ln(Q/K) > 0$ when $Q > K$, so the reverse reaction is favoured.\n(iii) Incorrect: a catalyst does not affect the equilibrium composition, so K and $\\Delta G^\\circ$ are unchanged.\n(iv) Incorrect: with $K < 1$ the forward reaction occurs only to a small degree; it is not stopped altogether.\n(v) Incorrect: at equilibrium it is $\\Delta G$ that becomes zero; $\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"In a 1 L sealed flask at a fixed temperature, $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$ is at equilibrium with $[H_2] = [I_2] = 0.10\\ M$ and $[HI] = 0.80\\ M$, so $K_c = 64$. Now 0.10 mol of $H_2$ is added at the same temperature. What is the new equilibrium concentration of HI?","image":"","options":["0.800 M","0.862 M","0.924 M","0.831 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: immediately after the addition, $[H_2] = 0.20$, $[I_2] = 0.10$ and $[HI] = 0.80$. Then $Q_c = 0.64/0.02 = 32 < 64$, so the reaction goes forward.\nLet x M of $H_2$ react: $\\frac{(0.80+2x)^2}{(0.20-x)(0.10-x)} = 64$\nThis gives $60x^2 - 22.4x + 0.64 = 0$. The chemically valid root is $x = 0.0312$.\nSo $[HI] = 0.80 + 2(0.0312) = 0.862\\ M$.\nThe new $[H_2] = 0.169\\ M$ lies between 0.10 and 0.20, as Le Chatelier's principle predicts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The $K_{sp}$ of AgCl at 298 K is $1.8 \\times 10^{-10}$. What is the molar solubility of AgCl in a 0.01 M NaCl solution? (Assume NaCl is completely dissociated.)","image":"","options":["$1.34 \\times 10^{-5}$ M","$1.8 \\times 10^{-8}$ M","$1.8 \\times 10^{-12}$ M","$1.34 \\times 10^{-7}$ M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $K_{sp} = 1.8 \\times 10^{-10}$, and the common ion $[Cl^-]$ from NaCl is 0.01 M.\nLet the solubility of AgCl be S. Then $[Ag^+] = S$ and $[Cl^-] = 0.01 + S \\approx 0.01$ M, since S is very small.\nFormula: $K_{sp} = [Ag^+][Cl^-]$\nSubstituting: $S = \\frac{1.8 \\times 10^{-10}}{0.01} = 1.8 \\times 10^{-8}$ M\nThis is much lower than the solubility in pure water ($1.34 \\times 10^{-5}$ M), which shows the common ion effect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"An acid–base indicator behaves as a weak acid:\n$HIn(aq) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons H_3O^+(aq) + In^-(aq)$\nHere $HIn$ shows colour A and $In^-$ shows colour B.\n(i) In a strongly acidic solution, colour A predominates.\n(ii) Adding NaOH shifts the equilibrium to the right, so colour B appears.\n(iii) The indicator acts as a weak base whose conjugate acid is $In^-$.\n(iv) $HIn$ and $In^-$ form a conjugate acid–base pair.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(ii) and (iii) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a high $[H_3O^+]$ pushes the equilibrium to the left, so $HIn$ (colour A) dominates.\n(ii) Correct: $OH^-$ removes $H_3O^+$, so the equilibrium shifts right and $In^-$ (colour B) appears.\n(iii) Incorrect: $HIn$ is a weak acid, and $In^-$ is its conjugate base, not its conjugate acid.\n(iv) Correct: $HIn$ and $In^-$ differ by exactly one proton.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): 1 mL of acetone placed in an open watch glass eventually evaporates completely, even though acetone in a closed vessel reaches a constant vapour pressure.\nReason (R): In an open system the vapour molecules disperse into a large volume, so the rate of condensation stays much less than the rate of evaporation and equilibrium cannot be reached.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT notes that liquids in open watch glasses eventually disappear completely.\nR is true: in the open, the rate of evaporation stays constant, but the vapour spreads through the room. Condensation therefore remains far slower than evaporation, so no equilibrium is set up.\nR directly explains why the liquid disappears completely in A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For the reaction $2SO_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2SO_3(g)$ in a sealed vessel at a fixed temperature, the equilibrium concentrations are $[SO_2]$ = 0.20 M, $[O_2]$ = 0.10 M and $[SO_3]$ = 0.60 M. What is $K_c$?","image":"","options":["30 $L\\,mol^{-1}$","9 $L\\,mol^{-1}$","90 $L\\,mol^{-1}$","0.0111 $L\\,mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $[SO_2]$ = 0.20 M, $[O_2]$ = 0.10 M, $[SO_3]$ = 0.60 M.\nFormula: $K_c = \\frac{[SO_3]^2}{[SO_2]^2[O_2]}$\nSubstituting: $K_c = \\frac{(0.60)^2}{(0.20)^2(0.10)} = \\frac{0.36}{0.004}$\nSo $K_c$ = 90 $L\\,mol^{-1}$.\nForgetting the squares gives 30; leaving out $[O_2]$ gives 9.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs is NOT a Brönsted conjugate acid-base pair?","image":"","options":["$HSO_4^-$ and $SO_4^{2-}$","$NH_3$ and $NH_2^-$","$H_3O^+$ and $OH^-$","$HCO_3^-$ and $CO_3^{2-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A conjugate acid-base pair differs by exactly one proton.\nCheck each pair:\n(1) $HSO_4^-$ → $SO_4^{2-}$ differs by one $H^+$, so it is a pair.\n(2) $NH_3$ → $NH_2^-$ differs by one $H^+$, so it is a pair.\n(4) $HCO_3^-$ → $CO_3^{2-}$ differs by one $H^+$, so it is a pair.\n(3) $H_3O^+$ and $OH^-$ differ by two protons. $H_3O^+$ pairs with $H_2O$, and $OH^-$ is the conjugate base of $H_2O$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (best description):\nList-I\nA. $BF_3$\nB. $HSO_4^-$\nC. $NH_2^-$\nD. $ClO_4^-$\nList-II\nI. Lewis acid that has no proton\nII. Species that can act as both a Brönsted acid and a Brönsted base\nIII. Very strong base, the conjugate base of a very weak acid\nIV. Very weak base, the conjugate base of a strong acid","image":"","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-I: BF$_3$ is electron deficient. It accepts a lone pair (for example from NH$_3$) even though it has no H$^+$ to donate.\nB-II: HSO$_4^-$ can gain a proton to give H$_2$SO$_4$ or lose one to give SO$_4^{2-}$, so it is amphiprotic.\nC-III: NH$_2^-$ is a very good proton acceptor, much stronger than H$_2$O.\nD-IV: HClO$_4$ is a strong acid, so its conjugate base ClO$_4^-$ is very weak.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species can act both as a Brönsted acid and as a Brönsted base?\n$H_2O$, $HCO_3^-$, $HSO_4^-$, $NH_3$, $NH_4^+$, $CO_3^{2-}$, $ClO_4^-$, $H_3O^+$","image":"","options":["3","5","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An amphiprotic species must be able both to donate and to accept a proton.\n$H_2O$ gives $H_3O^+$ or $OH^-$, $HCO_3^-$ gives $H_2CO_3$ or $CO_3^{2-}$, $HSO_4^-$ gives $H_2SO_4$ or $SO_4^{2-}$, and $NH_3$ gives $NH_4^+$ or $NH_2^-$. These 4 are amphiprotic.\n$NH_4^+$ and $H_3O^+$ act only as proton donors.\n$CO_3^{2-}$ and $ClO_4^-$ act only as proton acceptors, because they have no proton to give.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"At 300 K, a reaction mixture having reaction quotient $Q = 0.10$ has $\\Delta G = -2.00$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the equilibrium constant of the reaction at 300 K? (R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $e^{0.80} \\approx 2.23$)","image":"","options":["0.223","0.0448","2.23","22.3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Combine $\\Delta G = \\Delta G^\\circ + RT\\ln Q$ with $\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$. This gives $\\Delta G = RT\\ln(Q/K)$.\nSubstituting: $\\ln(Q/K) = -2000/(8.314 \\times 300) = -0.80$, so $K/Q = e^{0.80} = 2.23$.\nSo $K = 2.23 \\times 0.10 = 0.223$.\nThis is consistent: $\\Delta G < 0$ requires $Q < K$, and $0.10 < 0.223$.\nOption (2) wrongly takes $Q/K = 2.23$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the equilibrium\nAg$_2$O(s) + 2HNO$_3$(aq) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2AgNO$_3$(aq) + H$_2$O(l)\nWhich of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{AgNO}_3]^2[\\text{H}_2\\text{O}]}{[\\text{Ag}_2\\text{O}][\\text{HNO}_3]^2}$","$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{AgNO}_3]^2}{[\\text{HNO}_3]^2}$, and it has no units","$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{AgNO}_3]}{[\\text{HNO}_3]}$, because the coefficients cancel","$K_c = \\frac{[\\text{AgNO}_3]^2}{[\\text{HNO}_3]^2}$, with units mol L$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ag$_2$O(s) is a pure solid and H$_2$O(l) is a pure liquid. Both have constant concentrations and are omitted.\nThis leaves $K_c = [\\text{AgNO}_3]^2/[\\text{HNO}_3]^2$.\nThe numerator and denominator carry the same power of concentration, so the units cancel and $K_c$ has no units.\nOption (3) drops the stoichiometric powers. The value would be the same, but the expression no longer matches the balanced equation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) At a given temperature, an acid with a larger $K_a$ is a stronger acid.\n(ii) For a dibasic acid $H_2X$, the second ionization constant $K_{a2}$ is generally larger than $K_{a1}$.\n(iii) The $pK_a$ of HCN ($K_a = 4.9 \\times 10^{-10}$) is lower than that of HF ($K_a = 3.5 \\times 10^{-4}$).\n(iv) $K_a$ of acetic acid changes with temperature but not with the concentration of the acid.\n(v) The phenoxide ion is a stronger base than the acetate ion.","image":"","options":["2","4","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $K_a$ measures acid strength.\n(ii) Incorrect: it is harder to remove $H^+$ from the anion $HX^-$, so $K_{a1} > K_{a2}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: a smaller $K_a$ gives a larger $pK_a$, so HCN has the higher $pK_a$.\n(iv) Correct: an equilibrium constant depends only on temperature.\n(v) Correct: phenol ($K_a = 1.3 \\times 10^{-10}$) is a weaker acid than acetic acid, so its conjugate base is stronger.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Adding argon to an equilibrium mixture of gases kept at constant volume does not disturb the equilibrium.\nStatement II: For the reaction $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$, $K_p$ and $K_c$ have the same numerical value and no units.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. At constant volume, an inert gas changes neither the partial pressures nor the molar concentrations of the reacting species, so $Q_c$ is unchanged.\nStatement II is incorrect. Here $\\Delta n = 2 - 1 = 1$, so $K_p = K_c(RT)$. The two values differ, and $K_c$ has the unit mol/L while $K_p$ has the unit bar.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"9.2 g of $N_2O_4$ (molar mass 92 g/mol) is placed in an evacuated 1.0 L vessel at 400 K and allowed to reach the equilibrium $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$. The total pressure at equilibrium is 4.98 bar. Take R = 0.083 bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. What is $K_c$ for the reaction at 400 K?","image":"","options":["0.20","6.64","0.40","0.10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n = 9.2/92 = 0.10 mol, V = 1 L, T = 400 K.\nInitial pressure: $p_0 = nRT/V = 0.10 \\times 0.083 \\times 400 = 3.32$ bar.\nAt equilibrium: $p_{N_2O_4} = 3.32 - x$ and $p_{NO_2} = 2x$, so the total pressure is $3.32 + x = 4.98$, giving x = 1.66 bar.\nThen $K_p = \\frac{(3.32)^2}{1.66} = 6.64$.\nWith $\\Delta n = 1$: $K_c = \\frac{K_p}{RT} = \\frac{6.64}{33.2} = 0.20$.\nThe value 6.64 is $K_p$, not $K_c$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Using the ionization constants of weak acids at 298 K given in NCERT (Table 6.6), which of the following acids is the strongest?","image":"","options":["Acetic acid ($K_a = 1.74 \\times 10^{-5}$)","Benzoic acid ($K_a = 6.5 \\times 10^{-5}$)","Formic acid ($K_a = 1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$)","Hypochlorous acid ($K_a = 3.0 \\times 10^{-8}$)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At a given temperature, $K_a$ measures acid strength. The larger the $K_a$, the stronger the acid.\nCompare the values: $1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$ (formic) > $6.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ (benzoic) > $1.74 \\times 10^{-5}$ (acetic) > $3.0 \\times 10^{-8}$ (HOCl).\nBenzoic acid is the tempting choice, but $6.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ is smaller than $1.8 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species can act as Lewis acids?\n$BF_3$, $AlCl_3$, $Co^{3+}$, $Mg^{2+}$, $H_2O$, $NH_3$, $OH^-$, $H^+$, $F^-$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A Lewis acid accepts an electron pair.\nLewis acids: $BF_3$ and $AlCl_3$ are electron-deficient. $Co^{3+}$, $Mg^{2+}$ and $H^+$ are cations that can accept lone pairs. That makes 5.\nLewis bases: $H_2O$, $NH_3$, $OH^-$ and $F^-$ all have lone pairs to donate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the equilibrium constant $K_c$ of a reaction is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The value of $K_c$ does not depend on the initial concentrations of reactants and products.","For a reaction written as a particular balanced equation, $K_c$ has one unique value at a given temperature.","$K_c$ for the reverse reaction equals the reciprocal of $K_c$ for the forward reaction at the same temperature.","Increasing the concentration of a reactant at constant temperature increases the value of $K_c$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $K_c$ is the same whatever the starting amounts are.\n(2) Correct: $K_c$ depends on temperature and has one value for a given balanced equation at a given temperature.\n(3) Correct: $K'_c = \\frac{1}{K_c}$.\n(4) Incorrect: adding a reactant changes $Q_c$, not $K_c$. The system shifts until $Q_c$ again equals the same $K_c$. Only a change in temperature alters $K_c$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an example of a heterogeneous equilibrium?","image":"","options":["N$_2$(g) + 3H$_2$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2NH$_3$(g)","Fe$^{3+}$(aq) + SCN$^-$(aq) $\\rightleftharpoons$ Fe(SCN)$^{2+}$(aq)","H$_2$(g) + I$_2$(g) $\\rightleftharpoons$ 2HI(g)","CaCO$_3$(s) $\\rightleftharpoons$ CaO(s) + CO$_2$(g)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A heterogeneous equilibrium involves more than one phase.\nIn option (4), the thermal decomposition of CaCO$_3$ involves two solid phases and one gas phase, so it is heterogeneous.\nOptions (1) and (3) are entirely in the gas phase.\nOption (2) is entirely in solution, which NCERT cites as a homogeneous case.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For CaCO$_3$(s) $\\rightleftharpoons$ CaO(s) + CO$_2$(g) at a certain temperature, the equilibrium pressure of CO$_2$ is $0.75 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. Taking the standard pressure as 1 bar = $10^{5}$ Pa, what is the value of $K_p$?","image":"","options":["0.75","$7.5 \\times 10^{4}$","1.33","0.87"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_{CO_2} = 0.75 \\times 10^5$ Pa.\nFormula: the solids are omitted, so $K_p = p_{CO_2}$, with the pressure expressed in bar.\nSubstituting: $K_p = 0.75 \\times 10^5 / 10^5 = 0.75$.\nOption (2) forgets to convert Pa to bar. Option (3) is the reciprocal, 1/0.75.\nSo $K_p$ = 0.75.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a 0.1 M solution of a weak dibasic acid $H_2A$ with $K_{a1} = 1.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ and $K_{a2} = 1.0 \\times 10^{-10}$.\nStatement I: The concentration of $A^{2-}$ in this solution is numerically about $1.0 \\times 10^{-10}$ M.\nStatement II: Almost all the $H^+$ comes from the first ionization, so $[H^+] \\approx [HA^-]$, and these two terms cancel in the expression for $K_{a2}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct: $K_{a1} \\gg K_{a2}$, so the second step adds negligible $H^+$. The first step gives $[H^+] = [HA^-] = \\sqrt{10^{-5} \\times 0.1} = 10^{-3}$ M.\nStatement I is correct: $K_{a2} = \\frac{[H^+][A^{2-}]}{[HA^-]}$. Since $[H^+] \\approx [HA^-]$, this gives $[A^{2-}] \\approx K_{a2} = 1.0 \\times 10^{-10}$ M.\nBoth statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For which of the following gaseous equilibria are the numerical values of $K_p$ and $K_c$ equal at any temperature?","image":"","options":["$H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$","$N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g)$","$PCl_5(g) \\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g) + Cl_2(g)$","$N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Relation: $K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n}$, where $\\Delta n$ = moles of gaseous products − moles of gaseous reactants.\nFor $H_2 + I_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2HI$: $\\Delta n = 2 - 2 = 0$, so $(RT)^0 = 1$ and $K_p = K_c$.\nThe $NH_3$ synthesis has $\\Delta n = -2$. The $PCl_5$ and $N_2O_4$ dissociations each have $\\Delta n = +1$.\nIn these three cases $K_p \\neq K_c$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The equilibrium constants for four reactions at the stated temperatures are given below. For which reaction will the equilibrium mixture contain appreciable concentrations of both reactants and products?","image":"","options":["$H_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons H_2O(g)$, $K_c = 2.4 \\times 10^{47}$ at 500 K","$2H_2O(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2H_2(g) + O_2(g)$, $K_c = 4.1 \\times 10^{-48}$ at 500 K","$H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$, $K_c = 57.0$ at 700 K","$N_2(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO(g)$, $K_c = 4.8 \\times 10^{-31}$ at 298 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When $K_c$ lies between $10^{-3}$ and $10^{3}$, appreciable amounts of both reactants and products are present at equilibrium.\nOnly $K_c = 57.0$ falls in this range.\nWhen $K_c > 10^3$, as in (1), products predominate and the reaction goes nearly to completion.\nWhen $K_c < 10^{-3}$, as in (2) and (4), reactants predominate and very little product forms.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (salt solution of concentration c at 298 K) with List-II (expression for its pH):\nList-I:\n(A) NaCl\n(B) $CH_3COONa$\n(C) $NH_4Cl$\n(D) $CH_3COONH_4$\nList-II:\n(i) $7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a + \\log c)$\n(ii) $7 - \\frac{1}{2}(pK_b + \\log c)$\n(iii) $7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a - pK_b)$\n(iv) 7","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-i, C-ii, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) NaCl comes from a strong acid and a strong base. There is no hydrolysis, so pH = 7: (iv).\n(B) $CH_3COONa$ comes from a weak acid and a strong base. The solution is basic, with $pH = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a + \\log c)$: (i).\n(C) $NH_4Cl$ comes from a strong acid and a weak base. The solution is acidic, with $pH = 7 - \\frac{1}{2}(pK_b + \\log c)$: (ii).\n(D) $CH_3COONH_4$ comes from a weak acid and a weak base. Its pH is $7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a - pK_b)$ and does not depend on c: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g)$, $K_c = 50$ at a certain temperature. At some instant the concentrations are $[H_2] = 0.20$ M, $[I_2] = 0.10$ M and $[HI] = 0.50$ M. Which statement describes the mixture?","image":"","options":["$Q_c = 12.5$; net reaction proceeds in the forward direction","$Q_c = 12.5$; net reaction proceeds in the reverse direction","$Q_c = 25$; the mixture is already at equilibrium","$Q_c = 50$; no net reaction occurs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $Q_c = \\frac{[HI]^2}{[H_2][I_2]}$\nSubstituting: $Q_c = \\frac{(0.50)^2}{(0.20)(0.10)} = \\frac{0.25}{0.02} = 12.5$\nCompare with $K_c$: $Q_c = 12.5 < K_c = 50$.\nWhen $Q_c < K_c$, more products must form, so the net reaction goes left to right (forward).\nOption (2) has the right $Q_c$ but applies the comparison rule the wrong way round.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"When partial pressures are expressed in bar and concentrations in mol/L, what are the units of $K_p$ and $K_c$ respectively for $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g)$?","image":"","options":["bar and mol/L","$bar^{-1}$ and L/mol","Both are unitless","$bar^2$ and $(mol/L)^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$K_p = \\frac{(p_{NO_2})^2}{p_{N_2O_4}}$, which has units $\\frac{bar^2}{bar}$ = bar.\n$K_c = \\frac{[NO_2]^2}{[N_2O_4]}$, which has units $\\frac{(mol/L)^2}{mol/L}$ = mol/L.\nThe constants are unitless only when the powers in the numerator and denominator are equal, i.e. $\\Delta n = 0$. Here $\\Delta n = 2 - 1 = 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"The solubility product of barium sulphate at 298 K is $1.1 \\times 10^{-10}$. What is the molar solubility of $BaSO_4$ in pure water at this temperature?","image":"","options":["$1.05 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol/L","$5.5 \\times 10^{-11}$ mol/L","$3.02 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol/L","$2.10 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol/L"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_{sp}(BaSO_4) = 1.1 \\times 10^{-10}$; let the molar solubility be S.\nFormula: $BaSO_4(s) \\rightleftharpoons Ba^{2+} + SO_4^{2-}$, so $[Ba^{2+}] = [SO_4^{2-}] = S$ and $K_{sp} = S^2$.\nSubstituting: $S = \\sqrt{1.1 \\times 10^{-10}} = 1.05 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol/L.\nWhy the others fail: $3.02 \\times 10^{-4}$ comes from wrongly using $K_{sp} = 4S^3$, which applies to $AB_2$ salts. $5.5 \\times 10^{-11}$ is simply $K_{sp}/2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"A gas present in a reaction mixture has a molar concentration of 0.10 mol/L at 300 K. Using $p = cRT$ with $R = 0.0831$ bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, what is its partial pressure?","image":"","options":["2.49 bar","0.249 bar","24.9 bar","0.0249 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: c = 0.10 mol/L, T = 300 K, R = 0.0831 bar L mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $p = cRT$.\nSubstituting: p = 0.10 × 0.0831 × 300 = 2.493 bar.\nSo the partial pressure ≈ 2.49 bar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-EQUILIBRIUM-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Equilibrium","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Equilibrium","type":"mcq","question":"For a reaction mixture that has reached chemical equilibrium, which pair of conditions holds?","image":"","options":["$\\Delta G^\\circ = 0$ and $Q = 1$","$\\Delta G = 0$ and $Q = K$","$\\Delta G = \\Delta G^\\circ$ and $Q = 0$","$\\Delta G < 0$ and $Q < K$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At equilibrium no free energy is left to drive the reaction, so $\\Delta G = 0$, and the reaction quotient equals the equilibrium constant, $Q = K$.\nPutting these into $\\Delta G = \\Delta G^\\circ + RT\\ln Q$ gives $\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$.\nOption (1) is the tempting one. $\\Delta G^\\circ$ is a fixed standard value and is generally not zero at equilibrium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 6 · Equilibrium","difficulty":"easy"}]});
